leihawm = (t:n) (w:grotto/hole in the ground) (d:a hole or hollow in the ground where people hide or live) (e:suanghawm sung leh ~ sungah mite bu ding uh | people will hide in rock caves and in holes in the ground/~ leh keenhawm sung peuhah a teen’ uh kul | they had to live in holes in the ground and among the rocks) (r:23.2.19/18.30.6)
Leihawm = (t:n) (w:the Pit/pit) (d:leihawm written with a capital: the Pit, the deep place of the dead; Leihawm sungah paisuk is go down to the Pit) (e:~ sunga pai khinsate lak panin/~ thukna sungah nong koih khin hi | you have put me in the depths of the Pit/~ sungah paisukin ka sihna-ah bang metna om | what gain is there in my death, in my going down to the Pit/thuk penna ~ sungah hong kikoih suk ding hi | you will be put down into the Pit, the deepest place) (s:Leinuai/Leihawmpi) (b:leihawm) (r:19.30.3/23.14.15/19.88.6/19.30.9) (q:draft)
leihawmah = (t:adv) (w:to the cave/in the cave) (d:leihawm cave with the place marker ah) (e:kihemkhia-in Adullam ~ tai hi/Kei ka bukna ~ amaute hong tuaksuk uh | they come down on me in the cave where I shelter) (b:leihawm) (r:9.22.1/18.30.14) (q:draft)
