Leihawmpi = (t:n) (w:the Pit/grave/decay) (d:leihawmpi, a great hole in the earth, capitalised as the place of the dead where the body decays, named beside Leinuai) (e:na mi hoihpa ~ na mu sak kei hi/~ mu ngei lo zah ding, a hing tawntung | to live on for ever and never see the Pit) (s:Leihawm/Leinuai) (b:leihawmpi) (r:19.16.10/19.49.8) (q:draft)
leihawmte = (t:n) (w:caves/caverns) (d:leihawm, cave, with the plural te: caves and holes in the ground where people hide) (e:suanghawm sung, ~ sung, suangte sung/mualtung a ~ sungah bu uh a | they hid in the caves of the mountains/~ leh mun muanhuaite bawl uh hi | they made caves and strongholds/lo leh a sunga om ~ | the field and the cave in it) (s:suanghawmte) (b:leihawm) (r:9.13.6/7.6.2/66.6.15/1.23.20) (q:draft)
leihoih = (t:n) (w:good soil/fertile land) (d:lei, earth, with hoih, good: good soil in which seed grows well, fertile land) (e:logam ~ teng sehnel gam suak/~ mun khatah a suan hi | he planted it in a place of good soil/a tukna mun ~ tawh kibang uh hi | they are like the good soil on which the seed fell/na piak gamzai ~ ah omin | living in the broad and fertile land you gave) (s:leihoihna) (r:24.4.26/41.4.5/26.17.5/41.4.20/16.9.35) (q:draft)
leihoihna = (t:n) (w:good soil/good ground) (d:lei, soil, and hoih, good, with na: a place of good soil, fertile ground) (e:~ lak tu-in khang a/~ mun tu ahih manin hong po-in khang a | because it fell on good soil it came up and grew/limtakin tui a ngah theihna mun ~ sungah | in good soil where it could get plenty of water/Gamnuam ~ sungah amau guakin om uh hi | they live by themselves in fertile pastureland) (s:leihoih) (b:leihoih) (r:42.8.8/41.4.8/26.17.8/33.7.14) (q:draft)
leihumte = (t:n) (w:rough places/uneven ground) (d:uneven stretches of ground, which in the verse are to be made smooth) (e:a ~ kinelsak ding hi) (s:hamte) (r:42.3.5) (q:draft, low confidence)
