Naupaiina-ah = (t:n) (w:in childbearing) (d:naupaiina childbearing, pregnancy, with the place marker ah) (e:~ na gimna nakpitakin ka khangsak) (s:Naupaina-ah) (r:1.3.16) (q:draft)
naupaiite = (t:n) (w:pregnant women) (d:naupaii, be with child, with the plural te: pregnant women, often as nupi naupaiite) (e:~ le naungek neite a dingin/a nupi ~ uh kikuikham ding hi | their pregnant women will be ripped open) (s:naupaaite/gaite) (b:naupaii) (r:40.24.19/42.21.23/28.13.16) (q:draft)
naupaina = (t:n) (w:childbearing) (e:~-ah na gimna nakpi takin ka khangsak) (s:naupaiina) (b:naupaii) (r:1.3.16)
Naupaina = (t:see) <Naupaina-ah>
Naupaina-ah = (t:n) (w:in childbearing) (d:naupaina childbearing, pregnancy, with the place marker ah) (e:~ na gimna nakpi takin ka khangsak) (s:Naupaiina-ah) (r:1.3.16) (q:draft)
naupang = (t:n) (w:child) (e:numei leh ~ sim loh/Zeisu in ~ khat samin | Jesus called a child/Hih ~ ka ngen hi | I prayed for this child/a ~ Eli kiangah paipih uh hi | they brought the boy to Eli) (r:40.14.21/1.4.25/40.18.2/9.1.27/9.1.25) of offspring
naupangin = (t:n) (w:child) (d:naupang child with in: in a numei a naupangin, women and children alike; also naupang followed by the subject marker in, written as one word) (e:a numei a ~ susia-in/a si ~ nangma’ aa ahi hi | the dead child is yours) (b:naupang) (r:17.8.11/11.3.22) (q:draft)
naupangneu = (t:n) (w:small child/little boy/youth) (d:naupang child with neu small) (e:khuapi panin ~ pawlkhat hong paikhia-in) (s:naupangno) (r:12.2.23) (q:draft)
naupangno = (t:n) (w:little child/small child/young child) (d:naupang child with no small, young) (e:hih ~ bang na suah kei uh leh/amah tawh ~ khat pai hi | a small boy went with him/a ci-le-sa ~’ ci-le-sa bangin | his flesh like the flesh of a young boy) (s:naupangneu) (b:naupang) (r:40.18.3/9.20.35/12.5.14) (q:draft)
naupangnote = (t:n) (w:little children/young children/boys) (d:plural of naupangno, little child) (e:Tua ciangin ~ la-in a ang-ah tawi a/Hih ~ khatpeuh na simmawhkhak loh nadingun kidawm un | take care not to despise any of these little ones/~ sing kitomsakin a lelhpipi-un a kipuasak hi | boys stagger under loads of wood) (b:naupangno) (r:41.10.16/25.5.13/40.18.10) (q:draft)
naupangpa = (t:n) (w:boy/lad) (d:naupang child with pa, male: a boy, here a young servant) (e:Jonathan in a hiamte a ~ kiangah pia-in/Jonathan’ ~ in a thaltangte khawmin | Jonathan’s boy gathered the arrows) (b:naupang) (r:9.20.40/9.20.38) (q:draft)
naupangte = (t:n) (w:children) (d:plural of naupang, child) (e:hih ~ khatpeuh mah na simmawh/Nungak leh tangvalte, teeksiate leh ~ | young women and young men, old people and children/Amaute’ ulian dingin ~ ka bawl ding a | I will make children their officials/Tuuno bangin a ~ uh tai khia-in kimawl uh a | their children run out like lambs and play) (a:teeksiate) (b:naupang) (r:40.18.10/19.148.12/23.3.4/18.21.11)
nausawn = (t:n) (w:next younger brother) (d:nau, younger sibling, with sawn, in turn: the brother next in age) (e:a ~ in tenpih leuleu hi) (r:42.20.31) (q:draft, low confidence)
nausia = (t:v) (w:miscarry) (d:nau child with sia be spoiled: lose a child before birth; also of the woman to whom it happens) (e:kuamah ~ om lo ding a) (s:tatolh) (r:2.23.26) (q:draft)
nausuah = (t:v) (w:give birth/bear a child) (d:nau, child, with suah, bring out: give birth; also names childbirth, as in nausuah natna, birth pains, and nausuah hun, the time of delivery) (e:a ~ ding hun hong tung hi/Rachel a ~ hun tung a | Rachel’s time to give birth came/~ natna tungta ahih manin | because the pains of childbirth had come/~ natna bangin gimna thuak ding uh a | they will suffer pain like the pains of childbirth) (s:tasuah) (r:42.2.6/66.12.2/1.35.16/52.5.3) (q:draft)
nausuahna = (t:n) (w:childbirth/childbearing/delivery) (d:nausuah give birth with the noun suffix na; nausuahna tutphah is the delivery stool) (e:~ tutphah tungah a tu/~-ah gupna a ngah ding uh hi | they will be saved through childbearing) (s:nausuak) (b:nausuah) (r:2.1.16/54.2.15) (q:draft)
nausuak = (t:n) (w:labour/labor) (d:the labour of a woman giving birth; numei nausuak is a woman in labour) (e:numei ~ na a sa bangin/nupi ~ dingte bangin a lau ding uh hi | they will be afraid like women in labour/numei ~ ding bangin ka kikokhia ding a | I will cry out like a woman in labour) (r:19.48.6/24.48.41/23.42.14)
nausuak = (t:n) (w:childbirth) (e:~ gimna a tun’ ma-in/~ natna a thuak ma-in | before she suffers the pains of childbirth/~ gimna a thuak ngei lo numei aw | O woman who has never suffered the pains of childbirth) (s:nausuahna) (r:23.66.7/48.4.27)
nausuakte = (t:n) (w:women in labour/women giving birth) (d:nausuak, childbirth, with the plural te; numei nausuakte) (e:Numei ~ in na a sak) (b:nausuak) (r:24.13.21) (q:draft)
nauta = (t:n) (w:pupil of the eye/apple of the eye) (d:found in mit nauta, the pupil of the eye, said of what is guarded as most precious) (e:ama mit ~ bangin amaute kem hi/a mit ~ banga a na manpha pen | his most precious thing, like the pupil of his eye) (r:5.32.10/38.2.8) (q:draft)
nautang = (t:n) (w:common people/lay people/ordinary people) (d:used after mi as mi nautang, the people at large as distinct from priests and leaders) (e:mi ~ khempeuh kiangah tuate manlang takin/Tu-in mi ~ numei leh pasalte in | now the common people, men and women/mi ~ sungah a innkuan a pawl tawh kizui-in | among the lay people according to their family groups) (r:14.35.13/16.5.1/14.35.12) (q:draft)
nautangte = (t:n) (w:common people/lay people/ordinary people) (d:people without office or rank, often as mi nautangte) (e:mi ~ tungah ganhon panin tuunote/mi ~’ han tungah a vuite lawn hi | he scattered the dust over the graves of the common people/Israel ~, siampite, Levi mite | ordinary Israelites, priests and Levites) (s:minautangte) (b:nautang) (r:14.35.7/12.23.6/13.9.2) (q:draft)
naute = (t:n) (w:children) (e:sungah a ~ kilaigawp hi) (b:nau) (r:1.25.22)
nautum = (t:n) (w:youngest/youngest child) (d:the last-born of the brothers; a nautum pen is the youngest) (e:David in a ~ pen ahi hi/A ~ pen om lai hi | the youngest is still left) (r:9.17.14/9.16.11) (q:draft)
nauzaw = (t:n) (w:younger one/younger sibling/the younger) (d:the younger of two children or siblings, opposite of uzaw the older) (e:a ta ~ Jakob silhsak hi/A u’ ma-in a ~ piak ding | to give the younger before the older/a ~’ min Rachel ahi hi | the name of the younger was Rachel/a ta ~’ na sem ding hi | will serve the younger child) (b:nau) (r:1.27.15/45.9.12/1.29.26/1.29.16) (q:draft)
nauzawpa = (t:n) (w:younger son/younger brother/the younger) (d:nauzaw the younger with pa, a male) (e:a ~ Efraim’ lutang tungah nga a/a ~ in ama neihsateng zuakin | the younger son sold all he had) (s:naupa) (b:nauzaw) (r:1.48.14/42.15.13) (q:draft)
nauzawte = (t:n) (w:younger ones/younger men) (d:nauzaw, the younger, with the plural te) (e:Nang sangin a ~ na nau bangin) (s:khangnozawte) (b:nauzaw) (r:54.5.1) (q:draft)
navak = (t:adj) (w:quick-witted/bright/perceptive) (d:said of the mind, lungsim navak; lungsim navak lo is a dull or hardened heart) (e:lungsim ~ lo uh ahih manin/Na lungsim uh tua zahin ~ lo ahi hiam | are your hearts so dull) (r:41.6.52/27.1.4/41.8.17) (q:draft, low confidence)
navakna = (t:n) (w:insight/intelligence/keenness) (d:navak quick-witted with the noun suffix na; lungsim navakna is brightness of mind) (e:theihna leh lungsim ~ a nei hi) (b:navak) (r:27.5.11) (q:draft)
navakte = (t:n) (w:quick-witted ones/bright ones) (d:navak, quick of mind, with the plural te; lungsim navakte, those with a quick mind) (e:thei baih lungsim ~ ngentang ding) (b:navak) (r:27.1.4) (q:draft)
nawh = (t:v) (w:hurry/hasten/rush) (d:placed before another verb to say the action is done quickly or in haste, as nawh pai go swiftly and nawh nek eat in haste; ma-nawh is to head for a place) (e:Pumpeng gunkuang bangin ~ pai a/Laitaite sangin ka hun ~ tai zaw a | my days run swifter than a runner/na ~ nek ding uh hi | you shall eat it in haste/Amah hong ~ tai-in hong nai hi | he came running nearer and nearer/Egypt mite ~ tat mahmah uh hi | the Egyptians were very urgent) (s:kinawh) (a:zekai) (r:18.9.26/2.12.11/18.9.25/10.18.25/2.12.33) (q:draft)
nawh = (t:v) (w:drive/drive out/chase) (d:make an animal or people move on or away; nawh khia is to drive out) (e:Anak mite ~ khia siang hi/Gan ~ in | drive the animal on) (s:hawl) (r:6.11.21/12.4.24) (q:draft, low confidence)
nawhdelh = (t:v) (w:pursue/chase swiftly) (d:nawh, hurry or drive, with delh pursue) (e:Amah in amaute ~ lua kisa-in) (r:23.41.3) (q:draft)
nawhhawl = (t:v) (w:drive fast/drive furiously) (d:nawh hurry with hawl drive) (e:amah in nakpitakin ~ hi) (r:12.9.20) (q:draft)
nawhkeek = (t:v) (w:scatter/drive apart) (d:nawh drive with keek, apart) (e:kumpite a ~ ciangin) (s:nawhkek/thehthang) (a:kaikhawm) (r:19.68.14) (q:draft)
nawhkek = (t:v) (w:scatter/drive apart) (d:nawh drive with kek, apart) (e:kumpite a ~ ciangin) (s:nawhkeek/thehthang) (a:kaikhawm) (r:19.68.14) (q:draft)
nawhkhia = (t:v) (w:drive out/chase out/expel) (d:nawh, drive, with khia, out: drive or throw people out of a place) (e:gamah kong ~ ding hi/na mai-ah galte ~-in | driving out the enemies before you/a galte, khuamial sungah ~ hi | he drives his enemies into darkness) (s:hawlkhia) (b:nawh) (r:24.16.13/5.33.27/34.1.8) (q:draft)
nawhkhiat = (t:v) (w:drive out/expel) (d:form of nawhkhia used before khit ciangin and other following words) (e:amaute a ~ khit ciangin) (s:hawlkhiat) (b:nawhkhia) (r:5.9.4) (q:draft)
nawhkhiatna = (t:n) (w:driving out/expulsion) (d:nawhkhiat, drive out, with the noun suffix na) (e:na langte khempeuh uh ~ tawh) (s:hawlkhiatna) (b:nawhkhiat) (r:5.6.18) (q:draft)
nawhkhiatnate = (t:n) (w:things that drive away/acts of driving out) (d:nawhkhiat drive out with the noun suffix na and the plural te: the deeds that drive someone away) (e:a gamla semsemah kei hong ~ en in) (b:nawhkhiatna) (r:26.8.6) (q:draft)
nawhlen = (t:v) (w:swoop/fly swiftly) (d:nawh hurry with len, fly: said of an eagle swooping on its prey) (e:muvanlai sa bawh bangin a ~’ hi | it swoops like an eagle on its prey) (r:18.9.26) (q:draft, low confidence)
nawhpai = (t:v) (w:hurry/go quickly/speed past) (d:nawh, hurry, with pai, go: go in haste, speed along) (e:Amaute ~ mahmah uh a/Pumpeeng gunkuang bangin ka hun ~ a | my days speed by like boats of reed) (s:nawhtai/kinawh) (r:42.2.16/18.9.26) (q:draft)
nawhsa = (t:adv) (w:hastily/hurriedly/quickly) (d:always as nawhsa-in, in haste) (e:tua nu ~-in tai-in a pasal kiangah/Mite ~-in galkah uh hi | the people hurried across/Izipt gam panin ~-in na taikhia uh hi | you left Egypt in haste/~-in lopthop hihna in zawn’na lampi ahi hi | acting in careless haste is the road to poverty) (a:damdamin) (b:nawh) (r:7.13.10/20.21.5/6.4.10/5.16.3) (q:draft)
nawhsuk = (t:v) (w:chase down/rush down upon) (d:nawh, drive or hurry, with suk downward) (e:mualtung panin hong ~ uh a) (b:nawh) (r:25.4.19) (q:draft)
nawhtai = (t:v) (w:hurry/hasten/run swiftly/be swift) (d:nawh, hurry, with tai, run: run or move in great haste) (e:kei kiangah hong ~ ding uh hi/Laitaite sangin ka hun ~ zaw a | my days are swifter than a runner/Amah hong ~-in hong nai hi | he came hurrying nearer/kumpipa’ thu-kin sawl mah bangin ~ uh hi | they hurried off, sent by the king’s urgent command/Saul a pelh dingin David a ~ laitakin | as David was hurrying to get away from Saul) (s:nawhpai/nawhtat/kinawh) (a:zekai) (r:28.11.10/18.9.25/10.18.25/17.8.14/9.23.26) (q:draft)
nawhtaina = (t:n) (w:haste/hurried flight) (d:nawhtai, hurry, with the noun suffix na; a nawhtaina lamin, as she hurried away) (e:tua nu a ~ lamin naupang kia-in) (s:nawhtatna/kinawhna) (b:nawhtai) (r:10.4.4) (q:draft)
nawhtat = (t:v) (w:hurry/hasten/be urgent) (d:act in haste or press someone to make haste; of a task, be urgent) (e:Izipt mite ~ mahmah uh hi/kumpipa’ nasep in ~ ding kul ahih manin | because the king’s business was urgent/Saul pelh dingin David a ~ lai takin | as David was hurrying to get away from Saul/amahmah zong a paikhia dingin ~ hi | he himself hurried to go out) (s:nawhtai/kinawh) (a:zekai) (r:2.12.33/9.21.8/9.23.26/14.26.20) (q:draft)
nawhtatna = (t:n) (w:haste/hastiness/hurry) (d:nawhtat hurry with the noun suffix na; mawk nawhtatna is thoughtless haste) (e:mawk ~ in lungkhamna piangsak thei hi) (s:kinawhna/nawhtaina) (b:nawhtat) (r:20.19.2) (q:draft)
nawhzap = (t:v) (w:flap fast/beat rapidly) (d:nawh hurry with zap flap, of wings) (e:a kha uh bangzah takin ~ thei hiam) (r:18.39.13) (q:draft)
nawi = (t:n) (w:milk) (d:milk, of a mother or of an animal such as a cow, sheep or goat; nawi ne is to drink milk, to nurse) (e:~ bek a ne mi/Tuu ~ ne-in, tuumul silhin | you drink the milk of the sheep and wear the wool/ka keelte in ~ deih zahzah hong pia uh a | my goats gave me as much milk as I wanted/tuate in ~ tampi a piakna hangin | because they give plenty of milk) (s:nawitui) (r:58.5.13/26.34.3/18.29.6/23.7.22) (q:draft)
nawi = (t:n) (w:breast) (d:the breast of a mother; nawi tawh kikhen, to be parted from the breast, means to be weaned) (e:Isaak ~ tawh a kikhen ni/a ~ hong kiteepsak se ahi hiam | why was I given her breast to suck/A nu’ ~ tawh a kikhenta naupang bangin | like a child weaned from its mother’s breast/a note a ~ pia thei | they can give the breast to their young) (r:1.21.8/18.3.12/19.131.2/25.4.3) (q:draft)
nawine = (t:adj) (w:nursing/suckling) (d:feeding at the breast, nawi breast or milk with ne eat; nawine naungeek is a nursing baby) (e:nu in ~ naungeekte a khoi mah bangin/~ naungeek in gulgilo’ kuavangah kimawl ding a | the nursing infant will play by the cobra’s hole/~ lai naungekte zong paipih un | bring also the infants still at the breast/Gitlohna lamah ~ naungeek bang un | in evil be like nursing infants) (r:52.2.7/23.11.8/29.2.16/46.14.20) (q:draft)
nawine = (t:n) (w:infant/suckling) (d:a child still at the breast) (e:numei leh pasal, naupang leh ~/naupang leh ~ laite, bawngte leh tuu | children and infants, cattle and sheep) (s:naungek/naungeek) (r:9.22.19/9.15.3) (q:draft)
nawisuk = (t:adj) (w:milch/nursing) (d:of a female animal that is giving milk to her young, from nawi milk; kalaoh nawisuk, milch camels) (e:kalaoh ~ sawmthum le a note) (b:nawi) (r:1.32.15) (q:draft, low confidence)
nawite = (t:n) (w:breasts) (d:nawi, breast, with the plural te) (e:~ in thupha a ngah ding uh hi/ka ~ in a tausang ahi hi | my breasts are like towers/ama ~ neu mahmah hi | her breasts are very small) (b:nawi) (r:42.23.29/22.7.3/22.8.10/22.8.8) (q:draft)
nawitui = (t:n) (w:milk) (d:milk as a drink, from nawi, milk or breast, with tui, liquid; in the verse a picture of simple teaching for new believers) (e:~ ka hong tulh hi) (s:nawizu/nawi) (r:46.3.2) (q:draft)
nawizu = (t:n) (w:milk) (d:milk of a mother or an animal, as what the breast gives and as food; also a picture of simple teaching for new believers) (e:a pi’ ~ tawh keelno na huan/~ bek kong tulh hi | I fed you only milk/~ nete bek na hi uh hi | you are only milk drinkers/a ~ uh kangsak in | make their milk dry up) (s:nawitui/nawi) (r:2.23.19/46.3.2/58.5.12/28.9.14) (q:draft)
nawk = (t:v) (w:collide/strike/rush against) (d:strike or rush against something with force, as flood water against a house) (e:Hawktui in tua inn a ~ ciangin/siatna tuikhang in kei hong ~ hi | the torrents of destruction rushed against me/siatna tuihualte in hong ~ hi | the waves of destruction rushed against me/Tuikhang in tua inn a ~ ciangin | when the flood struck that house) (r:42.6.49/10.22.5/19.18.4)
nawk = (t:v) (w:bump/bang) (d:knock or strike against someone or something) (e:Lungkhamna in hong ~ niloh a | trouble keeps coming against me/Lungkhamna in huihpi’ ~ bangin | calamity, like the blast of a storm) (r:18.3.26/20.1.27)
nawkgawp = (t:v) (w:batter/beat against/lash/sweep over) (d:nawk, strike against, with gawp, violently: said of wind, flood, terror and anger that beat on someone or something) (e:tua inn a ~ uh hangin/Nangma hehna in keimah hong ~ khin a | your wrath has swept over me/tuikhang in ama maitang a ~ hangin | though the flood surges against its face/Lauhuaina in tuikhang pak bangin ~ ding a | terrors will overtake him like a flood) (b:nawk) (r:40.7.25/18.40.23/19.88.16/18.27.20) (q:draft)
nawkgawpin = (t:v) (w:batter/sweep over/overwhelm) (d:nawkgawp, beat violently against, with the ending in) (e:huihpi’ nawk bangin note hong ~) (b:nawkgawp) (r:20.1.27) (q:draft)
nawkin = (t:prep) (w:through/past) (d:nawk, pass through or pass by, with the linking ending in: going through a land or past a person) (e:Abram in tua gam ~ Shekhem mun/gamlak ~ na pai ciangin | when you marched through the wilderness/gamlak ~ ama mite a paipihpa tungah | to him who led his people through the wilderness/Gilead leh Manasseh ~ Gilead gam Mizpah ah | through Gilead and Manasseh to Mizpah of Gilead) (r:1.12.6/19.68.7/19.136.16/7.11.29)
nawkkha = (t:v) (w:pass by/go past/come past) (d:nawk, pass, with kha, happen to: come past a place or a person on the way) (e:hih inn a ~ khempeuh in lamdangsa-in/mihai pa’ leenggui lo ka va ~ hi | I went past the vineyard of a fool/mite in va ~ uh a | some people passed by/Na lote uh a ~ ngei mite’ muhna-ah | in the sight of those who passed by your fields) (s:paikha/kheng) (r:14.7.21/20.24.30/11.13.25/26.36.34) (q:draft)
nawkna = (t:n) (w:coming over/turning) (d:seen once beside kiheina, turning, of the Gentiles coming to God; nawk, go against or rush at, with the noun suffix na) (e:a ~ uh Foenisia gamah gen) (b:nawk) (r:44.15.3) (q:draft, low confidence)
nawkpai = (t:see) <nawkpai-in>
nawkpai-in = (t:v) (w:rush against/knock down) (d:nawk, bump against, with pai go and the ending in: pushing over someone while going) (e:amah ~ a tung sikin pai uh) (r:12.7.17) (q:draft, low confidence)
nawksak = (t:v) (w:let pass through/allow to cross) (d:nawk, pass through, with sak: permit someone to go through a land) (e:na gam sung hong ~ in/khe tawh hong ~ bek in | only let us pass through on foot/Khe tawh pai-in hong ~ un | let us pass through on foot/galte ka ~ kei ding hi | I will not let enemies pass through) (s:kantansak) (b:nawk) (r:7.11.17/5.2.27/5.2.28/4.20.19/38.9.8) (q:draft)
nawksuak = (t:v) (w:break through/press through/contend) (d:nawk push against with suak through: force a way through; upna thu-ah nawksuak is to struggle on for the faith) (e:Ammon mite’ gamgi ~ zo lo uh hi/Upna thu-ah hoihtakin ~ inla | fight the good fight of the faith/upna thu-ah note in na ~ nadingun | for you to contend for the faith) (r:4.21.24/54.6.12/65.1.3) (q:draft)
nawkzawh = (t:v) (w:be able to face/be able to go through) (d:nawk, go against, with zawh, form of zo be able) (e:hihte khempeuh ~ nading thahatna) (r:50.4.13) (q:draft, low confidence)
nawl = (t:n) (w:edge/border/outskirts) (d:the outer edge of a place or the area just outside it; khua nawl ah is outside the town) (e:na lo uh a ~ dongin/gamlak ~, Etham-ah giahphual sat uh hi | they camped at Etham on the edge of the desert/giahphual ~ ka tun’ ciangin | when I reach the edge of the camp/giahphual ~ kimkhat kangtum hi | it burned part of the outskirts of the camp) (s:gei/mong/pam) (r:3.19.9/2.13.20/7.7.17/4.11.1) (q:draft)
nawla = (t:n) (w:outside/outskirts/edge) (d:nawl, edge or outskirts, with the ending a, at) (e:khuapi ~ a biakinn kongah) (b:nawl) (r:44.14.13) (q:draft)
nawlah = (t:prep) (w:outside/at the edge of/beyond) (d:nawl edge with the place marker ah, at the edge or outside of a place) (e:khua ~ kote hong kha uh a/khua ~ a paipih khit ciangin | after he had led him outside the village/khua ~ a hawlkhiat khit uh ciangin | after they had driven him out of the city/Giahphual ~ hih a phakte hong tun’ uh ciangin | when these lepers came to the edge of the camp) (s:geiah) (a:sungah) (b:nawl) (r:44.21.5/40.21.39/41.8.23/44.7.58/12.7.8) (q:draft)
nawlhui = (t:n) (w:edge/edges) (d:the outer edge of the beard, khamul nawlhui; from nawl edge) (e:amau’ khamul ~ zong kiat loin) (r:3.21.5) (q:draft, low confidence)
nawlkhin = (t:v) (w:reject/refuse/turn away from) (d:push aside a person or his word and have nothing more to do with him) (e:Migilote in Pasian ~ uh a/amah na ~ uh a | they rejected him) (s:nial/nolh/nungngat) (a:saang) (r:18.21.14/44.7.39) (q:draft)
nawlkhinin = (t:v) (w:reject/let go/set aside) (d:nawlkhin, reject, with the ending in that links to the next verb) (e:Note in Pasian’ thukham ~) (s:nialin) (b:nawlkhin) (r:41.7.8) (q:draft)
nawlkhinzia = (t:n) (w:way of rejecting/way of setting aside) (d:nawlkhin, reject, with zia manner) (e:Pasian’ thukhamte a ~ na siam mahmah) (r:41.7.9) (q:draft)
nawlnung = (t:n) (w:far end/outer end/end) (d:the end or side furthest away; nawlnung pen is the outermost, said of the end curtain of a set) (e:inndei ~ kawm pua dong/a nihna puan ~ pen nawl-ah | on the edge of the end curtain of the second set/a inndei golzaw pen a ~ mahah omin | its portico was at the far end/a kikhuituah masa puan a ~ pen | the end curtain of the first set) (r:26.40.15/2.26.10/26.40.26/2.36.11) (q:draft)
nawm = (t:adv) (w:whole/down) (d:seen with valh swallow: nawm valh, swallow whole, gulp down) (e:gulpi bangin hong ~ valh hi) (r:24.51.34) (q:draft, low confidence)
nawmvalh = (t:v) (w:swallow/swallow up) (d:swallow, take in whole: said of the earth, the deep, a great fish and of enemies who destroy) (e:leitung in amaute ~ hi/Topa in hehna tawh amaute ~ ding a | the Lord will swallow them up in his wrath/eite a hingin hong ~ khin ding uh hi | they would have swallowed us alive/Jonah a ~ dingin | to swallow Jonah) (s:valh/valhtum) (a:luakhia) (r:2.15.12/19.21.9/19.124.3/32.1.17) (q:draft)
nawmvalhkha = (t:v) (w:swallow/happen to swallow) (d:nawmvalh, swallow, with kha, happen to or by mistake) (e:kalaoh a ~ tawh na kibang uh hi) (r:40.23.24) (q:draft)
nawmvalhna = (t:n) (w:swallowing/swallowing up) (d:nawmvalh swallow with the noun suffix na: the event of the earth swallowing them) (e:amaute a ~ ngaihsun un) (b:nawmvalh) (r:5.11.6) (q:draft)
nawmvalhsak = (t:v) (w:let swallow/cause to swallow) (d:nawmvalh, swallow, with the causative sak) (e:khuamial thukpi hong ~ kei in) (b:nawmvalh) (r:19.69.15) (q:draft)
nawmvalhtum = (t:v) (w:swallow up/swallow whole) (d:nawmvalh, swallow, with tum, entirely) (e:Amah ~ khin hang) (s:valhtum) (r:19.35.25) (q:draft)
nawn = (t:adv) (w:long/any longer/any more) (d:placed after a verb and before a negative such as lo or kei: no longer, not any more, not again) (e:hid ~ lo ding/aksite zong taang ~ lo hi | the stars no longer shine/lung khauh ~ kei un | do not be stubborn any longer/Jordan tuite luang ~ lo ding a | the waters of the Jordan will no longer flow/an hong piangsak ~ lo ding hi | it will no longer yield crops) (r:29.3.15/1.4.12/5.10.16/6.3.13) no longer
nawn = (t:v) (w:carry) (d:carry a load; seen in na a nawn mite, those who carried the building materials) (e:na a ~ mite in khut lang khat/na a ~ mite in a khut langkhat uh tawh | those who carried loads, with one hand) (s:pua) (r:16.4.17)
nawng = (t:n) (w:marsh/swamp/mire) (d:wet, muddy ground where reeds grow) (e:~ sunga pumpeng lakah bu hi/tuipi dunga om ~ lakte | the swamps along the sea) (s:ciknawng/nawngpi) (r:18.40.21/26.47.11) (q:draft)
nawngkai = (t:v) (w:be troubled/be disturbed/be hindered) (d:nawngkai sa is feel disturbed or annoyed; nawngkai sak is to trouble, stir up or hinder) (e:thu hilh uh ahih manin ~ a sa uh hi/a ~ sak uh hi | they stirred up trouble/Topa ~ sak lo ding hi | nothing will hinder the Lord) (s:buai) (r:44.4.2/9.14.6/44.17.13) (q:draft)
nawngkaina = (t:n) (w:hindrance/disturbance/trouble) (d:nawngkai be troubled or hindered with the noun suffix na) (e:~ khat beek om loin/i khua sungah ~ hong piangsak hi | they are causing trouble in our city) (s:buaina) (b:nawngkai) (r:44.28.31/44.16.20) (q:draft)
nawngkaisa = (t:v) (w:be annoyed/feel troubled) (d:nawngkai, be troubled, with sa feel) (e:Paul in ~ ahih manin) (b:nawngkai) (r:44.16.18) (q:draft)
nawngkaisak = (t:v) (w:trouble/bother/disturb/hinder) (d:cause trouble or difficulty to someone: nawngkai with the causative sak) (e:Ka pa in gamsung ~ hi/Bang hangin ~ na hi uh hiam | why are you bothering her/hih meigongnu in hong ~ niloh ahih manin | because this widow keeps bothering me/Israel a ~ Akhar ahi hi | Achar, who brought trouble on Israel) (s:buaisak/lungkhamsak) (b:nawngkai) (r:9.14.29/41.14.6/42.18.5/13.2.7) (q:draft)
nawngkaisakin = (t:v) (w:trouble/hinder/put obstacles before) (d:nawngkaisak, trouble or hinder, with the ending in that links to the next verb) (e:mite’ upna ~ pawl a kikhensak mite) (b:nawngkaisak) (r:45.16.17) (q:draft)
nawngkaisakte = (t:n) (w:troublers/those who trouble) (d:nawngkaisak, trouble or disturb, with the plural te: the ones who trouble) (e:note hong ~ pen Pasian in) (s:lungkhamsakte) (b:nawngkaisak) (r:48.5.10) (q:draft)
nawngkaisakzo = (t:v) (w:be able to hinder/be able to trouble) (d:nawngkaisak, hinder, with zo be able) (e:nang hong ~ ding bangmah om lo) (b:nawngkaisak) (r:20.4.12) (q:draft)
nawnglakte = (t:n) (w:swamps/marshes) (d:nawng marsh with lak, among, and the plural te: marshy places) (e:tuipi dunga om ~ le) (r:26.47.11) (q:draft)
nawngpi = (t:n) (w:marsh/swamp/great marsh) (d:nawng marsh with pi great: wet ground where grass and reeds grow) (e:omna ngei leitang pen ~ suakin) (s:ciknawng/nawng) (b:nawng) (r:23.35.7) (q:draft)
nawnkei = (t:adv) (w:no longer/not any more/not again) (d:nawn any more with the negative kei, written as one word after a verb; usually written nawn kei) (e:tua zanin ging ~ hen/Ka thuakzo ~ a, ka genkhia ta ding hi | I can bear it no longer, I will speak out/a donkik ~ ding hi | he will not build them up again/cikmah ciangin na ngahkik ~ ding hi | you will never get them back again) (s:nawnlo) (r:18.3.7/18.32.20/19.28.5/66.18.14) (q:draft)
nawnkhia = (t:v) (w:remove/carry out/take away) (d:nawn, carry, with khia out) (e:siangtho mun panin ~ un) (s:puakhia) (b:nawn) (r:14.29.5) (q:draft)
nawnlo = (t:adv) (w:no longer/no more/not any more) (d:nawn any longer with the negative lo, written as one word after a verb; usually written nawn lo) (e:piak kikkik ding kisam ~ ding/Kei tungah Pasian heh ~ taktak hi | God is truly no longer angry with me/lungsim kisuan’na om ~ ding ahih manin | because there would be no more sense of guilt) (s:nawnkei) (b:nawn) (r:58.10.2/24.2.35) (q:draft)
nawnloin = (t:adv) (w:no longer/without any more) (d:nawnlo no longer, with the linking ending in: no longer doing so, and) (e:uh lampi lui zui ~/mawh nei lo mite that ~ | no longer killing innocent people/na meihal biaknate uh hal ~ | no longer burning your burnt offerings) (b:nawnlo) (r:24.18.15/24.7.6/24.7.21) (q:draft)
nawt = (t:v) (w:gore/push/thrust) (d:push hard against something, as an ox with its horns; nawt gawp is treat roughly, assault) (e:hihte tawh Siria mite na ~ ding hi/amah in tuate tawh mite ~ ding a | with them he will gore the peoples/hawktui in tua inn a ~ hangin | though the torrent struck that house/A pa ~ gawpin | treating his father violently) (s:pik/not) (r:14.18.10/20.19.26/5.33.17/42.6.48) (q:draft)
nawt = (t:v) (w:press forward/advance/march on) (d:with mai face: mai nawt, push ahead, go forward) (e:Thakhauh takin mai ka ~ suak ding hi/Sakolte aw, mai ~ un | charge, you horses/tang takin mai hong ~ uh a | they march straight ahead/puahkikna nasep in mai ~ a | the repair work went forward) (s:mainawt/maipai) (a:nungkik) (r:7.5.21/24.46.9/29.2.7/14.24.13) (q:draft)
nawt = (t:v) (w:wear away/rub off/scrape/wash away) (d:rub or scrape against a surface so that it wears off, usually with khia out; also shape wood with a scraping tool) (e:guahtui in lei a ~ khiat bangin/Tui in suangte ~ khia-in | as water wears away stones/amah in singnawtna tawh ~ a | he shapes it with a plane) (r:18.14.19/23.44.13) (q:draft, low confidence)
nawtgawpin = (t:v) (w:treat violently/assault/mistreat) (d:nawt, push or thrust, with gawp, violently, and the ending in) (e:A pa ~ a nu a notkhia) (r:20.19.26) (q:draft)
nawtin = (t:adv) (w:onward/forward/pressing on) (d:nawt, push on, with the linking ending in; mai nawtin pai is go forward) (e:Israel mite mai lam ~ pai uh hi/Mai ~ pai un | go forward/Israel mite mai ~ a pai ding uh gen in | tell the Israelites to move on/amaute mai ~ pai-in | they pressed forward) (b:nawt) (r:2.40.36/6.6.7/2.14.15/12.3.24) (q:draft)
nawtkhia = (t:v) (w:push out/drive out/thrust out/depose) (d:nawt, push, with khia, out) (e:a hon lak panin na ~ uh hi/Na nasepna phual panin nang kong ~ ding a | I will depose you from your office/Amah in na mai-ah galte ~-in | he drives out the enemy before you/a galte khempeuh khuamial sungah a ~ hi | he drives all his foes into darkness) (s:hawlkhia/nawhkhia/delhkhia) (b:nawt) (r:26.34.21/23.22.19/5.33.27/34.1.8) (q:draft)
nawtkhiat = (t:v) (w:wear away/wash away) (d:nawt, rub or wear, with khiat, form of khia out, used before bangin and other following words) (e:guahtui in lei a ~ bangin) (s:sawpkhiat) (b:nawt) (r:18.14.19) (q:draft)
nawtsakzaw = (t:v) (w:advance further/cause to advance more) (d:nawt, press forward, with the causative sak and zaw more; mai nawtsakzaw, make something go forward all the more) (e:lungdamna thu mai a ~ ahihna) (r:50.1.12) (q:draft)
nawtsuak = (t:v) (w:press on/go right through/march on) (d:nawt push forward with suak through to the end; mai nawtsuak is march on) (e:Thakhauh takin mai ~ ding/a maban uh ~ uh hen la | let them go the whole way) (r:7.5.21/48.5.12) (q:draft)
nawtsuakin = (t:v) (w:press on/go right on) (d:nawtsuak, press on to the end, with the ending in that links to the next verb) (e:ka ma ~ ka pai ding hi) (b:nawtsuak) (r:42.13.33) (q:draft)
nawtsuk = (t:v) (w:sweep down/push down into) (d:nawt push forward with suk downward: of flood water, press on down into a land) (e:Judah gam dong hong ~ ding a) (b:nawt) (r:23.8.8) (q:draft)
Nazareth = (t:name) (c:city) (w:Nazareth) (d:town in Galilee where Jesus grew up) (r:42.1.26/42.18.37)
NAZARETH = (t:name) (c:city) (w:Nazareth) (d:Nazareth, the home town of Jesus in Galilee, written in capitals in the notice on the cross) (r:43.19.19) (q:draft)
nazat = (t:n) (w:equipment/utensils/furnishings/articles) (d:na, thing, with zat, use: the things used with something, such as the utensils of the altar and the furnishings of the tabernacle) (e:meihal biakna tau a ~ khempeuh tawh/amau’ nasepna dinga ~ khempeuh pua ding uh hi | they shall carry all the equipment for their service/sabuai leh a ~ khempeuhte | the table and all its articles/kikhopna buk sung ~ khempeuh vaan ding uh a | they shall take care of all the furnishings of the tent of meeting) (r:2.30.28/4.4.26/2.39.36/4.3.8)
nazatte = (t:n) (w:utensils/articles/accessories/furnishings) (d:nazat, equipment, with the plural te: the utensils and furnishings that belong to the altar, the table or the lampstand) (e:hih ~ khempeuh kibawl ding hi/meihal biakna tau a ~ khempeuh tawh | the altar of burnt offering with all its utensils/sabuai a zawlte leh a ~ khempeuh tawh | the table with its poles and all its articles/meivakkhuam leh a ~ | the lampstand and its accessories) (s:umlebeelte/vanzatte) (b:nazat) (r:2.25.39/2.31.9/2.35.13/2.30.27) (q:draft)
nazaw = (t:v) (w:hurt more/be harder) (d:na, be painful, with zaw more: it is the more painful or hard for someone) (e:nang a dingin ~ hi) (r:44.26.14) (q:draft, low confidence)
Nazereth = (t:name) (c:city) (w:Nazareth) (d:spelling of Nazareth in one verse; Nazereth mi is a Nazarene, a person from Nazareth) (r:40.2.23)
Nazir = (t:name) (w:Nazirite) (d:a person set apart for God by a special vow, who drinks no wine and does not cut the hair; Nazir mi is a Nazirite) (r:4.6.13/30.2.12)
nbsp = (t:todo) (q:draft, not a word, the HTML entity nbsp left in the Bible text)
ne = (t:v) (w:eat) (d:take food; an ne is eat a meal; the form nek or neek is used before ding, theih and nading) (e:siang khempeuh na ~ thei ding uh hi/A kihhuai na khatpeuh peuh na ~ kei ding uh hi | you shall not eat any detestable thing/tho in, go in la, ~ in | get up, kill and eat/lungnuamin an a ~ uh hi | they were encouraged and ate food/Cinapa in an ~ zo lo a | the sick man cannot eat food) (s:anne) (r:5.14.11/1.2.17/5.14.3/44.10.13/44.27.36/18.33.20)
