nisagih = (t:n) (w:week) (d:ni day, with sagih seven: seven days) (e:~ laka ni masa bel ni-in) (s:nipikal) (r:46.16.2) (q:draft)
Nisan = (t:name) (w:Nisan) (d:first month of the Hebrew year, in spring, the month of the Passover) (r:16.2.1/17.3.7)
nisat = (t:n) (w:heat of the day/heat of the sun) (d:form of nisa; sun nisat hun is the hot part of the day around noon, nisat hun the hot season) (e:sun ~ dong Ammonte vat tuk uh/sun ~ hun pawlin Ishbosheth’ inn-ah | to the house of Ish-Bosheth about the heat of the day/sun ~ laitak, tu-in a om lai takin | while he was sitting in the heat of the day/anlak ~ hun sunga meiizun bangin | like a cloud of dew in the heat of harvest) (s:nisa) (b:nisa) (r:9.11.11/10.4.5/1.18.1/23.18.4) (q:draft)
nisawm = (t:n) (w:ten days) (d:ni, day, with sawm, ten; nisawm-le-nih is twelve days, and with ni after it the day of the month, nisawm leh nili ni the fourteenth day) (e:~ sung etcik dingin a thukim hi/Kha masa a ~ ni-in | on the tenth day of the first month/khathum leh ~ sung uk hi | he reigned three months and ten days/amah tawh ~ leh ninga ka omkhawm hi | I stayed with him fifteen days) (r:27.1.14/27.1.15/6.4.19/14.36.9/48.1.18) (q:draft)
nisim = (t:adv) (w:daily/every day/regularly) (d:ni day with sim, each; also placed before a noun as in nisim nasep, daily work) (e:na ~ nasep uh zo un/~ a nasep uh a sem dingin | to do their work every day/~ biak piaknate khawlsak ding uh a | they will stop the daily sacrifices/~ mahin sihna a thuakthuak ka hi hi | I face death every single day/meigongte in ~ kivakna a ngah loh manun | because the widows did not get the daily share of food) (s:nisimin) (r:2.5.13/58.9.6/27.11.31/46.15.31/44.6.1) (q:draft)
nisima = (t:adv) (w:daily/of each day/every day) (d:nisim, daily, with the linking a) (e:~ a kisam bangin/~ maizum den dingin | to be in shame every day) (b:nisim) (r:13.16.37/24.20.18) (q:draft)
nisimin = (t:adv) (w:daily/every day) (d:nisim, daily, with the adverb ending in: every day, day after day) (e:~ biakna innpi sungah tu a/~ khat leh khat kihansuah un | encourage one another daily/tua nu’ innkuan ading ~ an om hi | there was food every day for the household of the woman/~ ka kong gei-ah omin | being at my doors every day/pawlpi khempeuh lunghihmawhna tawh ~ gimna ka thuak hi | I suffer daily from concern for all the churches) (s:nisim) (r:40.26.55/26.43.25/58.3.13/11.17.15/20.8.34/47.11.28) (q:draft)
Nisrok = (t:name) (w:Nisrok) (d:god of the Assyrians, in whose temple King Sennacherib was killed by his sons) (r:12.19.37/23.37.38)
nisuah = (t:n) (w:sunrise) (d:ni, sun, with suah, come out; the time when the sun rises, set against nitum, sunset) (e:~ leh nitum kikal nai sawm/~ lai takin hanah a pai uh hi | at sunrise they went to the tomb/~ khit ahihleh tua sihna hangin mawhna om ding hi | if it is after sunrise, there is guilt for that death) (s:nisuak) (a:nitum) (r:43.11.9/41.16.2/2.22.1) (q:draft)
nisuahna = (t:n) (c:direction) (w:east) (d:the side where the sun rises, nisuah sunrise with the noun suffix na; nisuahna lam is the east side or eastward) (e:~ lamah kihei in la/~ lam mite’ gam tung hi | he came to the land of the eastern peoples/~ lam kongpi tawnin biakinn sungah lut hi | it entered the temple through the east gate/Amaute ~ lamah lal uh a | they moved eastward) (s:suahna) (a:nitumna) (b:nisuah) (r:11.17.3/1.2.8/1.29.1/26.43.4/1.11.2)
Nisuahna = (t:n) (c:direction) (w:east) (d:written with a capital in the Bible text, as in Nisuahna lam mite, the peoples of the East, and Nisuahna Kongpi, the East Gate) (e:Ahi zongin ~ lam ka zong a/~ leh nitumna gamte panin | from the countries of the east and the west/Midian mite leh Amalek mite leh ~ lam mite | the Midianites, the Amalekites and the eastern peoples/~ lam na gamgi uh | your eastern boundary) (a:Nitumna) (b:nisuahna) (r:18.23.8/16.3.29/38.8.7/7.6.3/4.34.10) (q:draft)
nisuak = (t:n) (w:sunrise/dawn/rising sun) (d:ni, sun, with suak, come out: the rising sun or the time when it rises; nisuak tung is the moment of sunrise) (e:a mittangte ~ bangin a taang hi/zingsang ~ bangin thak tawntunga | always new like the morning sunrise/nisuahna lam nga-in ~ tung na bia uh hi | facing east they were worshipping the rising sun) (s:nisuah) (a:nitum) (r:18.41.18/25.3.23/26.8.16) (q:draft)
nisuh = (t:n) (c:plant) (w:palm/palm tree/date palm) (d:nisuh kung is the tree and nisuh gah its fruit) (e:Thuman mi in ~ kung bangin palh a/~ gah bawk pha tawh kibang hi | they are like clusters of palm fruit/tua tungah ~ leh khainiang bawl hi | on it he made palm and chain designs/amaute in ~ khuapi luah uh hi | they took possession of the City of Palms) (r:19.92.12/5.34.3/22.7.7/14.3.5/7.3.13) (q:draft)
nisuhkung = (t:n) (c:plant) (w:palm tree) (d:nisuh palm, with kung tree) (e:Bethel kikal ~ nuai-ah tu zel a) (s:Tumkung) (r:7.4.5) (q:draft)
nisuhte = (t:n) (c:plant) (w:palms/palm trees) (d:nisuh, palm, with the plural ending te) (e:Mang kawlsingte, ~, leh apple kungte-a kipan) (b:nisuh) (r:29.1.12) (q:draft)
nit = (t:adj) (w:unclean/defiled/filthy) (d:dirty or ceremonially unclean, the form of nin used before words such as hun, sung, sak and lohna; a nit hun, the time of her uncleanness, is a woman's monthly period) (e:a ~ lohna mun om lo hi/a ~ hun sunga a lupna peuhpeuh | anything she lies on during her period/ka biakinn a ~ sak nadingun | in order to defile my house/Israel mite’ ~ sakna panin siangtho sak ding | he shall consecrate it from the uncleanness of the Israelites) (s:nin) (a:siang) (r:23.28.8/3.18.19/3.15.20/24.7.30/3.16.19) (q:draft)
nitaang = (t:n) (w:sunshine/sunlight) (d:the light of the sun; sun nitaang is the bright sun of midday) (e:i mit in ~ a muh ciangin/Amah in ~ sungah a khang kungno | he is a young plant growing in the sunshine/khataang pen ~ bang ding a | the moonlight will be like the sunlight/zan khuamial pen sun ~ tawh kibang hi | the dark of night is like the bright day) (r:21.11.7/18.8.16/23.30.26/19.139.12) (q:draft)
nitak = (t:n) (w:evening) (d:the end of the day, from late afternoon until dark; nitak ciangin is in the evening, and nitak nai thum is three in the afternoon) (e:~ mateng na sem uh hi/~ ciangin vulin keu uh hi | by evening it withers and dries up/a puan sawpin, ~ mateng a nin hi ding hi | he must wash his clothes and be unclean till evening/~ nai nga lai takin a cialte | those hired about five in the afternoon) (a:zingsang) (r:19.104.23/1.8.11/19.90.6/3.11.25/40.20.9)
Nitak = (t:see) <Nitak-an>
Nitak-an = (t:n) (w:supper/evening meal) (d:nitak evening with an food; Topa’ Nitak-an is the Lord's Supper) (e:Topa’ ~ ahi kei hi) (r:46.11.20) (q:draft)
nitakin = (t:adv) (w:in the evening/at twilight) (d:nitak evening with the ending -in; tu nitakin means tonight) (e:zing leh ~ Filistiapa hong pai-in/tuuno khat pen ~ na pia ding uh hi | the other lamb you shall offer at twilight/~ a ngahsa teng hawm hi | in the evening he divides what he has taken/Aaron in ~ zong meivakte a koih ciangin | when Aaron sets up the lamps at twilight) (a:zingsangin) (b:nitak) (r:9.17.16/4.28.4/1.49.27/2.30.8) (q:draft)
nitawp = (t:n) (w:last day/end time) (d:ni, day, with tawp, end; nearly always nitawp ni, the last day, the day of resurrection and judgment) (e:~ ni ciangin ka hingsak ding hi/~ ni ciangin na ngah ding uh hotkhiatna | the salvation you will receive at the last day/~ ni hun pen a lauhuai hun | the time of the last days is a terrible time) (r:43.6.54/55.3.1/60.1.5) (q:draft)
nite = (t:n) (w:days) (d:ni with plural -te; sabbath nite, khadet nite and pawi nite are the Sabbaths, New Moon days and feast days) (e:Note in ~, khate, hunte, kumte na tang/na gam ukna ~ hong sim sak a | he has numbered the days of your reign/ni siangtho ~ leh sabbath ~ a beisak hi | he has put an end to the holy days and the Sabbaths/Note’ khadet ~ leh na pawite uh ka mudah hi | I hate your New Moon days and your festivals) (b:ni) (r:48.4.10/13.23.31/27.5.26/25.2.6/23.1.14) (q:draft)
niteh = (t:part) (d:sentence-final word in reported thought, it must be so, surely) (e:mudah taktak hi ~ ci-in ka ngaihsun hi) (r:7.15.2) (q:draft, low confidence)
nithum = (t:n) (w:three days) (d:ni, day, with thum, three; nithum sung is for three days) (e:~ sunga a lam kik dingpa aw/David tawh ~ sung an ne tui dawnin | eating and drinking with David for three days/amah tawh ~ sung om hi | he stayed with him three days/~ leh nilang tawntung a en ding uh hi | they will gaze for three and a half days) (r:41.15.29/6.3.2/13.12.39/7.19.4/66.11.9) (q:draft)
nithum = (t:n) (w:third day) (d:followed by ni, nithum ni, the third day) (e:~ ni-in a thokik hi/a ~ ni-in biakpiakna sa a om lai teng | whatever meat of the sacrifice is left on the third day) (r:46.15.4/3.7.17) (q:draft)
nitna = (t:n) (w:uncleanness/impurity/defilement) (d:nit, unclean, with the noun suffix na: the state of being ritually unclean, or an unclean act) (e:ama tungah ~ om napi-in/ama sungah ~ mah hi veve hi | it is still uncleanness in him/tua in ~ ahi hi | that is an act of impurity/amaute’ ~ leh a gitlohna uh tawh kituakin | according to their uncleanness and their wickedness) (s:ninna) (a:siangna) (b:nit) (r:3.7.20/3.15.3/3.20.21/26.39.24) (q:draft)
nitnateng = (t:n) (w:all uncleanness/all impurity) (d:nitna uncleanness, with teng all) (e:mawhna le ~ khempeuh a silsiang dingin) (b:nitna) (r:38.13.1) (q:draft)
nitsak = (t:v) (w:defile/make unclean/desecrate) (d:nit, unclean, with the causative sak: make a person, a place or a holy thing unclean) (e:a ki-ap ama sam a ~ leh/note in amah na ~ uh ciangin | when you defile it/amau’ giahphual a ~ loh nadingun | so that they will not defile their camp/Na ~ uhleh na si ding uh hi | if you defile them you will die) (s:ninsak) (a:siangsak/siansak) (b:nit) (r:4.6.9/3.18.28/4.5.3/4.18.32) (q:draft)
nitsakna = (t:n) (w:defilement/desecration/profaning) (d:act of making something unclean) (e:note in sabbath ~ tawh Israel tungah/gamsung teng a ~ hangun | because they defiled the whole land/ka biakbuk ~ hangin amau’ nitna sungah | in their uncleanness for defiling my dwelling place) (b:nitsak) (r:16.13.18/3.15.31/26.36.18) (q:draft)
nitsan = (t:v) (w:sniff at/blow the nose at) (d:with nap, nasal mucus: nit, blow the nose, with san, toward someone; a gesture of contempt) (e:nap nong ~ lel uh hi) (r:39.1.13) (q:draft, low confidence)
nitum = (t:n) (w:sunset/sundown) (d:ni sun with tum set, the time when the sun goes down) (e:hanciamin ~ dong a ngaihsun hi/~ ma-in ama’ tungah na piakik ding hi | you shall return it to him before sunset/~ laitakin amah si hi | at sunset he died/~ mateng ama’ khutte kip hi | his hands stayed steady till sunset) (a:nisuah) (r:27.6.14/2.22.26/14.18.34/2.17.12) (q:draft)
nitumna = (t:n) (c:direction) (w:west) (d:the place of sunset, from ni sun and tum set) (e:Nisuahna leh ~ gamte panin/~ lam biakbuk nungah giahphual satin | camping on the west, behind the tabernacle/Nisuahna pan ~ dongin Topa’ min phat ding | the name of the Lord is to be praised from east to west/~ lam, ni a tum sukna lam | westward, toward the setting sun) (s:tumna) (a:nisuahna) (r:38.8.7/19.113.3/4.3.23/5.11.30) (q:draft)
Nitumna = (t:n) (c:direction) (w:west) (d:Nitumna Tuipi, the western sea, is the Mediterranean Sea) (e:Judah gam khempeuh ~ Tuipi dong/~ lam ka zong a | I search toward the west/~ gamgi dingin Tuipi Lian | the Great Sea as the western boundary) (a:Nisuahna) (b:nitumna) (r:5.34.2/18.23.8/4.34.6) (q:draft)
nitumte = (t:n) (w:idlers/drowsy ones) (d:nitum, sunset, with te, those who: those who only eat and let the day go by till sunset; the English has drowsiness) (e:a ne bek-a ~ in puansia bek) (b:nitum) (r:20.23.21) (q:draft, low confidence)
nizang = (t:n) (w:sunbeam/ray of the sun/sunshine) (d:ni, sun, with zang; the light that the sun sends out) (e:tua in ~ bangin note tungah cidamna) (r:39.4.2) (q:draft, low confidence)
no = (t:pron) (w:you) (d:second person plural pronoun; before a noun it addresses a group, as in no tuucingte aw, you shepherds, and before kong or hong it is the object of the verb) (e:Tua ahih manin ~ tuucingte aw/Tu ni-a ~ kong piak hih thute | these words that I give you today/tuuno kikhahte bangin ~ kong sawl hi | I send you out like lambs set loose/khua mite in ~ hong nolh leh | if the people of the town reject you) (s:nang/note) (r:26.34.9/5.6.6/42.10.3/42.10.10) used for only the plural
No = (t:pron) (w:you) (d:no, you plural, written with a capital at the start of a sentence: it addresses a group or is the object of the verb) (e:~ kong khiasuk veve ding hi/~ Levi mite aw, tu-in za un | now listen, you Levites/~ hong bawlsiate thupha na pia un | bless those who persecute you/~ nangawn hong kheem zo ahi hiam | has he deceived even you) (b:no) (r:31.1.4/4.16.8/45.12.14/43.7.47) (q:draft)
Noadiah = (t:name) (c:male/female) (w:Noadiah) (d:name of two people: a Levite, son of Binnui, who helped weigh the temple treasures in the time of Ezra, and a prophetess who tried to frighten Nehemiah) (r:15.8.33/16.6.14)
noah = (t:see) <noah-taaksing>
Noah = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Noah) (d:son of Lamech, who built the ark and with his family survived the flood) (r:1.5.29/1.7.5)
noah-taak = (t:n) (c:plant) (w:cypress/gopher wood) (d:the tree whose wood was used for the ark; noah-taak sing is its wood) (e:Nang a dingin ~ sing tawh teembaw) (r:1.6.14) (q:draft, low confidence)
noah-taaksing = (t:n) (c:plant) (w:cypress wood/gopher wood) (d:noah-taak with sing wood: the wood used for the ark) (e:Nang adingin ~ tawh teembaw khat bawl in) (r:1.6.14) (q:draft, low confidence)
noahtaak = (t:n) (c:plant) (w:gopher) (d:the tree whose wood was used for the ark of Noah; written noah-taak in the Bible text)
Noahte = (t:name) (c:female) (w:Noah) (d:Noah with the plural te, Noah and the others: one of the five daughters of Zelophehad of the tribe of Manasseh) (r:4.36.11)
Nob = (t:name) (c:city) (w:Nob) (d:town of the priests in Benjamin near Jerusalem, where Ahimelek the priest helped David) (e:~ khua-ah galte tungin khawl uh a) (r:23.10.32/9.22.9) (q:draft)
Nobah = (t:name) (c:male/place) (w:Nobah) (d:man of Manasseh who captured Kenath and named it Nobah after himself; also a place east of the Jordan near Jogbehah) (r:4.32.42/7.8.11)
Nobo = (t:name) (c:city) (w:Nebo) (d:town of Moab named in the judgment on Moab, spelled Nebo in the NIV) (r:24.48.22)
Nod = (t:name) (c:land) (w:Nod) (d:land east of Eden where Cain settled) (r:1.4.16)
Nodab = (t:name) (c:people) (w:Nodab) (d:people east of the Jordan, defeated together with the Hagrites by Reuben, Gad and the half tribe of Manasseh) (r:13.5.19)
Nogah = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Nogah) (d:son of David born in Jerusalem) (r:13.3.7/13.14.6)
noh = (t:v) (w:caress/fondle) (d:touch a wife or husband lovingly) (e:Rebekah a ~ laitak mu kha hi) (r:1.26.8) (q:draft)
Nohah = (t:name) (c:male/place) (w:Nohah) (d:fourth son of Benjamin; in Judges 20:43 the Tedim text also has Nohah as a place near Gibeah) (r:7.20.43/13.8.2)
nokik = (t:v) (w:sprout anew/grow fresh again) (d:of grass, come up young again, with kik again) (e:zingsangin a ~ lopa tawh kibang uh) (r:19.90.5) (q:draft, low confidence)
nokkhak = (t:v) (w:hinder/hold back/trouble) (d:get in the way of what someone does; nokkhak sak is detain or weary someone) (e:kua in hong ~ zo ding ahi hiam/nang kong ~ sak loh nadingin | so that I do not weary you) (s:khaktan) (r:23.43.13/44.24.4) (q:draft, low confidence)
noleno = (t:pron) (w:yourselves) (d:no, you plural, said twice and joined by le, and; used with a verb in ki for what people do to themselves) (e:~ kisuliam ding na hi uh hiam/~ a kininsaktawm na hi uh hi | you defile yourselves) (r:24.47.5/26.20.31) (q:draft)
nolh = (t:v) (w:reject/refuse/turn away) (d:refuse to accept or welcome a person or thing) (e:khua mite in no hong ~ leh/tua ama tate a ~ hi | he rejected those children of his/amah in siatna ~ lo hi | he does not reject evil/keima thungetna hong ~ loin | not rejecting my prayer/kumpi hihna panin hong ~ khin hi | he has rejected you as king) (s:nial) (a:saang) (r:42.10.10/5.32.19/19.36.4/19.66.20/9.15.26) (q:draft)
nolhkhitna = (t:n) (w:rejection/having rejected) (d:nolh reject, with khit finished and the noun suffix na: the fact of having rejected) (e:panin amah ka ~ mu napi-in) (r:9.16.1) (q:draft)
nolhte = (t:n) (w:those who reject/whoever rejects) (d:nolh, reject, with te, those who) (e:Note hong ~ in kei zong hong nolh hi) (s:nialte) (b:nolh) (r:42.10.16) (q:draft)
nom = (t:n) (w:moisture/dampness) (d:wetness in the soil; in the list of plagues in Deuteronomy 28:22 a nom stands where the NIV has mildew) (e:leisung a ~ tam lo ahih manin) (r:42.8.6/5.28.22) (q:draft)
nomau = (t:pron) (w:yourselves/you/your own) (d:second person plural, emphatic or reflexive; with aa or the possessive mark, yours) (e:~ mah na kigawt ding uh hi/pasiante ~ adingin bawl kei un | do not make gods for yourselves/a ki-ap na peuhmah ~ aa hi ding hi | every devoted thing shall be yours/~ nasem suak uh hen | let him become your servant/Tu-in ~ kisian’suah un la | now consecrate yourselves) (r:3.16.31/4.18.14/3.19.4/41.10.43/14.29.5) (q:draft)
nomsak = (t:v) (w:moisten/wet/make damp) (d:nom with the causative sak; of dew wetting branches and of clouds filled with moisture) (e:daitui in a ~ tawntung singkung/Amah in meiipi ~ a | he loads the clouds with moisture) (s:kawtsak) (a:keusak) (b:nom) (r:18.29.19/18.37.11) (q:draft)
nona = (t:n) (d:no, young or tender, with the noun suffix na; in lo nona, a place of fresh young grass, green pasture or grassland) (e:Lo ~-ah keimah hong lumsak a/gamlak lo ~ munte adingin thuum un | lament for the grasslands of the wilderness/lo ~-a nuam taka om tuute | sheep living at ease in green pasture) (b:no) (r:19.23.2/24.9.10/24.50.44) (q:draft)
nong = (t:pron) (w:you) (d:second person subject marker used when the action is directed at the speaker, you to me or you to us, placed before the verb) (e:~ piak an leh tui/Kei ~ it uh leh | if you love me/lau lo dingin thu ~ pia hi | you told me not to fear/Ke’n kong dot leh, ~ dawng kik kei ding uh hi | if I asked you, you would not answer me) (r:43.16.31/1.3.12/43.14.15/25.3.57/42.22.68)
Nong = (t:pron) (w:you) (d:second person subject acting on the speaker, you to me, placed before the verb) (e:~ thuhilh khit ciangin dah-in ka kun/~ dawn’ theih leh hong dawng in | answer me if you can/~ hotkhiat ding ka ngakngakna-in ka tha bei ta hi | my strength fails from waiting for you to save me/~ vuakna ma ka tung panin lakhia in | take away from me the wound of your blow) (b:nong) (r:24.31.19/18.33.5/19.119.81/19.39.10) (q:draft)
nongeekte = (t:n) (w:chicks/young ones) (d:no, young, with ngeek, tender, and the plural ending te: the very young of a bird or animal, in the verses the chicks of a hen) (e:akpi in a ~ a kha nuai-ah) (r:40.23.37/42.13.34) (q:draft)
nongek = (t:n) (w:tender young/suckling young) (d:no young of an animal, with ngek tender; a nongek neite is those nursing their young) (e:a ~ neite ka lungphamawh hi) (r:1.33.13) (q:draft)
nono = (t:part) (d:placed after a descriptive word to speak of several things that each have that quality, as hoih nono fine ones and anlim nono choice foods) (e:nisimin puan manpha ~ silhin/anlim ~ ne-in a om hi | he lived eating choice foods/amau’ adingin inn hoih ~ a lam | who built fine houses for themselves/letsong hoih ~ a namkim la hi | he took all kinds of fine gifts/bangbang thu haksa ~ a dot hangin | whatever hard matters he asked about) (r:42.16.19/18.3.14/1.24.10/27.1.20) (q:draft)
nonote = (t:part) (d:nono with the plural ending te, placed after a descriptive word for several things that each have that quality; haksa nonote, the hard ones) (e:thu dotna haksa ~) (b:nono) (r:27.5.12) (q:draft)
nop = (t:v) (w:want/wish/desire) (d:form of nuam used after another verb in dependent clauses, want to, be willing to) (e:Topa a mat ~ uh hangin/ka gamtat ~ lohna ka gamtat leh | if I do what I do not want to do/amah in tua ganhing a tat ~ leh | if he wishes to redeem the animal/A pha ka gamtat ~ ka hih thei kei hi | the good I want to do I cannot do) (s:nuam) (r:43.7.44/45.7.16/3.27.13/45.7.19) (q:draft)
nopci = (t:adj) (w:pleasant/delightful) (d:of a land that is good to live in) (e:gam ~ khat nang hong pia ning) (s:noptuak) (r:24.3.19) (q:draft)
