puazaw = (t:v) (w:take on rather/bear instead) (d:pua, carry or bear, with zaw, rather: nasem lim a hong puazaw is he took on the form of a servant instead) (e:nasem lim a hong ~ hi) (b:pua) (r:50.2.7) (q:draft, low confidence)
puazosak = (t:v) (w:enable to carry/make able to bear) (d:pua, carry, with zo, be able, and the causative sak: make able to carry) (e:tui meii a ~ hi) (r:18.26.8) (q:draft)
Publias = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Publius) (d:chief official of the island of Malta, whose sick father Paul healed) (r:44.28.7/44.28.8)
Pudens = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Pudens) (d:Christian in Rome who sent greetings to Timothy through Paul) (r:55.4.21)
puk = (t:v) (w:fall/stumble/fall down) (d:fall down or stumble, of people, animals and things; also fall in battle and, in a figure, fall into sin; puk nading is a cause of stumbling) (e:ka khe nuai-ah ~ uh hi/namsau tawh note na ~ ding uh hi | you will fall by the sword/ama mai-ah ~ uh a | they threw themselves down before him/~ dingin ka kithawi a | I am about to fall/ama tungah ~ nading a om kei hi | there is nothing in him to make him stumble) (s:tuk/kia) (a:tho) (r:19.18.38/62.2.10/4.14.43/1.50.18/19.38.17) (q:draft)
pukcip = (t:v) (w:collapse/fall in a heap) (d:puk, fall, with cip, completely: collapse) (e:guh nei lo bangin ka ~ suk hi) (b:puk) (r:35.3.16) (q:draft)
pukcipsuk = (t:v) (w:collapse/sink down) (d:pukcip, collapse, with suk, downward: sink down in a heap) (e:guh nei lo bangin ka ~ hi) (b:pukcip) (r:35.3.16) (q:draft)
pukding = (t:v) (w:will stumble/going to fall) (d:puk, stumble, with ding for what is to come, written as one word) (e:mi khat peuhpeuh a ~ ahihleh) (b:puk) (r:45.14.20) (q:draft)
pukgawp = (t:v) (w:fall down/collapse) (d:puk fall with gawp, severely or completely: fall right down) (e:Amaute kinawmsawmin ~ ding uh a/hong si-in ~ ding hi | he will fall down dead) (b:puk) (r:19.20.8/44.28.6) (q:draft)
pukin = (t:v) (w:falling/fall/fall down/stumble) (d:puk with the linking ending in, followed by another verb: falling, fell and) (e:ama namsau tungah ~ si hi/~ thokik nawn lo ding uh hi | they will fall and never rise again/nangma mai-ah amaute ~ kisia uh hi | they stumble and perish before you/Mualte ~, keente cimin | the mountains fall and the cliffs crumble) (b:puk) (r:13.10.5/1.50.1/30.8.14/19.9.3/26.38.20) (q:draft)
pukkha = (t:v) (w:stumble/trip/fall) (d:puk, fall, with kha, happen to: stumble over something or trip; in a figure, fall into sin) (e:zong na ~ kei ding hi/khuavak sungah lampai leh ~ cih om lo hi | whoever walks in the light does not stumble/mawhna-ah a ~ a om ciangin | when someone falls into sin/tungah a ~ uh ahi hi | they stumbled over it) (r:20.4.12/45.9.32/43.11.9/47.11.29) (q:draft)
pukkhak = (t:v) (w:fall/stumble/fall away) (d:fall by mishap, in the verses of falling away from faith; mostly in pukkhak loh nadingin, so as not to fall) (e:Note na ~ loh nadingun/ze-etna-ah na ~ loh nadingun thu ngen un | pray so that you will not fall into temptation/amah a ~ loh nadingin | so that he will not fall/a ~ loh nadingun kikem uh hen | let them take care that they do not fall) (b:pukkha) (r:43.16.1/46.10.12/42.22.46/46.8.13) (q:draft)
pukkhawm = (t:v) (w:fall together) (d:puk, fall, with khawm, together) (e:a nihun a ~ uh hi/singkung liante tawh ~ ding hi | it will fall together with the great trees/nangmah leh Judah mite na ~ nadingun | so that you and Judah fall together) (s:kiakhawm/tukkhawm) (b:puk) (r:24.46.12/14.25.19/23.10.34) (q:draft)
pukkhin = (t:v) (w:have fallen/be fallen) (d:puk, fall, with khin, already or completely; also of people who have all fallen away) (e:taamtaak kungte ~ a/Amaute khempeuh a vekpi-un ~ uh hi | they have all fallen away together) (s:kiakhin) (b:puk) (r:38.11.2/19.14.3) (q:draft)
puklawh = (t:v) (w:fall by/be brought down/collapse) (d:fall or collapse as the result of something, such as one's own wickedness or neglect) (e:amau’ gitloh mah ~ uh hi/a innpi nangawn in ~ ding hi | even his great house will collapse/Amah in a ngaihsut khialhna ~ hi | he falls by his own wrong schemes/Midangte in a ~ theih annekna | eating by which others may fall) (r:20.11.5/21.10.18/18.18.7/45.14.21) (q:draft)
pukna = (t:n) (w:fall/downfall/defeat) (d:puk, fall, with the noun suffix na: a fall, downfall or defeat, and in a figure a fall into sin) (e:kiphatsakna in ~ hong piang sak hi/Ka galte’ ~ ka mitte in mu khin a | my eyes have seen the fall of my enemies/A ~ uh gin’ ngaih lua ahih manin | because the sound of their fall is so loud/amaute a ~ hangin Gentailte in gupna a ngah | because of their fall the Gentiles have received salvation) (s:tukna/kiatna) (b:puk) (r:20.16.18/19.92.11/24.49.21/45.11.11) (q:draft)
puknah = (t:n) (c:plant) (w:hyssop) (d:a small bushy plant used with cedar wood and scarlet yarn in rites of cleansing) (e:taamtaak sing leh ~ leh a ausan khaute/a ausan puan leh ~ a la dingin | to take scarlet yarn and hyssop/a ausan khau leh ~ tawh la khawmin | taking them together with scarlet yarn and hyssop) (r:4.19.6/3.14.4/3.14.49)
puknahte = (t:n) (c:plant) (w:hyssop/hyssop plants) (d:puknah, hyssop, with the plural te: the hyssop plants that grow out of a wall) (e:a po ~ dongin) (b:puknah) (r:11.4.33) (q:draft)
pukpah = (t:v) (w:fall away at once/fall quickly) (d:puk, fall, with pah, at once: fall away immediately when trouble comes) (e:a tun ciangin a ~ uh hi) (r:41.4.17) (q:draft)
pukpih = (t:v) (w:stumble with/fall with) (d:puk, fall, with pih, together with: fall or stumble along with someone) (e:Judah mite in zong ~ ding uh hi) (b:puk) (r:28.5.5) (q:draft)
puksak = (t:v) (w:cause to fall/cause to stumble/offend) (d:puk with the causative sak: make someone fall, also make someone stumble in faith) (e:Na khut in hong ~ leh/Hih thu in note hong ~ hiam | does this make you stumble/ka u ka nau ka ~ loh nadingin | so that I do not make my brother fall/naupang khat a ~ mi peuhmah in | whoever makes one child stumble/Amau’ mawhna in amaute ~ ding a | their sin will make them fall) (s:tuksak/khialhsak) (b:puk) (r:41.9.43/3.26.7/43.6.61/46.8.13/41.9.42/28.5.5) (q:draft)
puksakin = (t:v) (w:throwing down/making fall) (d:puksak, cause to fall, with the linking ending in: throwing someone down and then doing the next action) (e:naupang lei ah ~ a kongkawsak hi) (s:tuksakin) (b:puksak) (r:42.9.42) (q:draft)
puksakkha = (t:see) <puksakkha-in>
puksakkha-in = (t:v) (w:happen to make fall/cause to stumble) (d:puksak cause to fall with kha, which shows that the action may come about, and the linking ending in) (e:ze-etna in note hong ~) (r:52.3.5) (q:draft)
puksakna = (t:n) (w:being struck down/knocking down) (d:puksak cause to fall with the noun suffix na; puksakna thuak, be struck down) (e:~ ka thuak uh hangin) (b:puksak) (r:47.4.9) (q:draft)
puksakpa = (t:n) (w:one who causes to stumble/offender) (d:puksak, cause to fall, with pa marking a man: the man through whom others are made to stumble) (e:a ~ in gimna a thuak ding hi/Hih naupangte khatpeuh a ~ in | the one who makes any of these little ones stumble) (b:puksak) (r:40.18.7/42.17.1/42.17.2) (q:draft)
puksakte = (t:n) (w:those who cause to stumble/causes of sin) (d:puksak cause to fall with plural te) (e:a khialte leh a ~ khempeuh) (b:puksak) (r:40.13.41) (q:draft)
puksakzo = (t:v) (w:be able to bring down/have thrown down) (d:puksak cause to fall, with zo, be able to or have done) (e:Kua in eite hong ~ ding ahi hiam/meikhuk sung le tui sungah tamveipi ~ hi | it has often thrown him into fire and into water) (b:puksak) (r:31.1.3/41.9.22) (q:draft)
puksi = (t:v) (w:starve/die of hunger/perish) (d:puk, fall, with si, die: fall down dead; in the verses always of dying from hunger) (e:kial-in a ~ ding uh hi/an ngawlin a ~ veve ding ahi hi | he will surely starve to death without food/A om lai sunte gilkialin ~ ding uh hi | those who remain will die of famine/Kongzing khempeuh ah naupangte ~ khin dektak uh hi | at every street the children are about to die of hunger) (r:24.11.22/25.4.9/24.38.9/26.6.12/25.2.19) (q:draft)
puksih = (t:v) (w:die of hunger/starve/perish) (d:form of puksi used before sangin, ding and similar words; puksih ding stands for famine in Job 5:22) (e:Nek ding nei lo-a ~ sangin/Suksiat ding leh ~ dingte na nuihsan lel ding | you will laugh at destruction and famine) (b:puksi) (r:25.4.9/18.5.22) (q:draft)
puksisak = (t:v) (w:starve/starve to death/cause to perish) (d:make someone fall dead, in the verses always by famine; puksi with the causative sak) (e:kial tawh ka ~ ding hi/a tate uh kial tawh ~ inla | give their children over to famine/mihon buppi a ~ dingin | to starve this whole assembly to death/na tate uh gilkialin ~ nawn lo ding hi | it will no longer starve your children) (b:puksi) (r:24.44.12/26.5.16/24.18.21/2.16.3/26.36.12) (q:draft)
puksite = (t:n) (w:the dead/those who have perished) (d:puksi fall dead with the plural te; kialin a puksite, those who have died of famine) (e:kialin a ~ bekbek ka mu hi) (b:puksi) (r:24.14.18) (q:draft)
puksuk = (t:v) (w:fall down/fall/slump down) (d:puk fall with suk downward: fall to the ground, also fall facedown before God) (e:Amah in lei tungah ~ a/a leeng sungah amah ~ hi | he slumped down in his chariot/lei maingatin ~ uh hi | they fell facedown/amah in a mai-in lei-ah ~ a | she bowed down with her face to the ground) (b:puk) (r:44.9.4/12.9.24/3.9.24/8.2.10) (q:draft)
puksukin = (t:v) (w:bowing down/bending down) (d:puksuk, fall or bend downward, with the linking ending in; a lu puksukin, bowing his head) (e:a lu ~ a si ta hi/Bel pasian ~, Nebo pasian kunsuk a | the god Bel bows down, the god Nebo stoops) (s:kunsukin) (b:puksuk) (r:43.19.30/23.46.1) (q:draft)
puksukpah = (t:v) (w:fall down at once/drop down) (d:puk fall with suk down and pah at once) (e:thakhatin si-in ~ a) (r:44.5.5) (q:draft)
pukta = (t:v) (w:has fallen/is fallen) (d:puk fall with ta, which shows that the event has now taken place) (e:Kumpi gilo pa ~ a) (b:puk) (r:23.14.4) (q:draft)
pukte = (t:n) (w:those who fall/the fallen) (d:puk, fall, with plural te: people who fall or fall away; sat pukte, those who struck someone down) (e:thakhatin a ~ ahi hi) (b:puk) (r:40.13.21/23.28.1) (q:draft)
pukthei = (t:v) (w:can stumble/may fall) (d:puk stumble with thei can) (e:khuavak nei lo ahih manin ~ hi) (b:puk) (r:43.11.10) (q:draft)
puktheih = (t:v) (w:can stumble/may fall) (d:form of puk thei, be able to fall, used before nading; a puktheih nading, something that can make a person stumble) (e:mite a ~ nading suang khat/a ~ nading thu om lo hi | there is nothing to make them stumble/Mite a ~ nading ze-etna hong piang hamtang ding hi | temptations that make people stumble are sure to come) (b:puk) (r:45.9.33/62.2.10/42.17.1) (q:draft)
puktheihna = (t:n) (w:stumbling/cause of stumbling) (d:puktheih can stumble with the noun suffix na; puktheihna ding suangtum, a stone that makes people stumble) (e:mite’ ~ ding suangtum tawh kibang hi) (b:puktheih) (r:23.8.14) (q:draft)
pul = (t:n) (w:plague/pestilence) (d:deadly disease that spreads among many people, also in pul natna) (e:kial leh ~ hong tung ding hi/kialpi tawh ahi zongin, ~ natna tawh ahi zongin | whether by famine or by plague/a si dingte ~-in si ding uh a | those who are to die will die of plague) (s:pulnatna/pulpi) (r:42.21.11/24.42.17/24.15.2) (q:draft)
Pul = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Pul) (d:king of Assyria, also called Tiglath-Pileser, who invaded Israel in the days of Menahem) (e:Assiria kumpipa ~ tua gam do dingin) (r:12.15.19/13.5.26) (q:draft)
Pul = (t:n) (w:plague/pestilence) (d:pul, plague, with a capital at the start of a sentence; pul natna, a deadly epidemic disease) (e:~ natna tawh amaute vat gawpin) (s:pulnatna/pulpi) (b:pul) (r:4.14.12) (q:draft)
pulak = (t:v) (w:confess/declare/acknowledge/make known) (d:make something known openly: confess a sin or wrong, or declare the deeds of God among people) (e:Ka thuman lohna ka ~ a/amah in a gamtatnasa a khialhna ~ ding a | he must confess the wrong he has done/mi khempeuh lakah ~ un | declare it among all peoples/Ka khialhnate Topa tungah ka ~ ding hi | I will confess my transgressions to the Lord/ka pute ka pate’ gitlohna uh kong ~ uh hi | we acknowledge the wickedness of our ancestors) (s:genkhia/taangko) (a:im/seel) (r:19.38.18/18.32.17/3.5.5/19.96.3/19.32.5/24.14.20) (q:draft)
pulakin = (t:v) (w:confessing/declaring) (d:connective form of pulak, confessing or making known openly, followed by another verb) (e:amau mawhna khialhna ~ Jordan gun sungah/~ a gamtatna thu a gen uh hi | they openly told what they had done/Tuate ~ gen ding hi leng | if I were to declare and tell of them/ka mipih Israelte’ mawhna ~ | confessing the sin of my people Israel) (b:pulak) (r:40.3.6/16.9.3/44.19.18/19.40.5/27.9.20) (q:draft)
pulakkhia = (t:v) (w:utter/speak out/declare) (d:pulak, make known, with khia, out: speak out, utter) (e:kua’ lungsim hong ~ na hi hiam) (s:genkhia) (b:pulak) (r:18.26.4) (q:draft)
pulaklak = (t:v) (w:keep declaring/keep making known/reveal) (d:pulak make known, with the last syllable doubled for an action that goes on and on, parallel to gengen, keep speaking) (e:zan simin theihtelna ~ hi) (r:19.19.2) (q:draft)
pulakna = (t:n) (w:declaration/revelation/announcement) (d:pulak make known, with the noun suffix na; pulakna bawl, make a declaration) (e:na nasempa tungah ~ na bawl khin hi) (b:pulak) (r:10.7.27) (q:draft)
pulaknop = (t:v) (w:want to declare/wish to confess) (d:pulak declare with nop, the form of nuam want used before a noun) (e:nangma kiangah kong ~ thu in) (r:44.24.14) (q:draft)
pulaksa = (t:v) (w:confessed/declared) (d:pulak confess with sa already: what has been confessed or declared) (e:mi tampi’ zakin na ~) (b:pulak) (r:54.6.12) (q:draft)
pulaksak = (t:v) (w:cause to declare/let mention/make proclaim) (d:pulak, declare, with the causative sak) (e:Baal pasian min ka ~ nawn kei ding/Amah gensakin ~ unla | let him speak and declare it) (b:pulak) (r:28.2.17/23.44.7) (q:draft)
pulepa = (t:n) (w:ancestor/forefather) (d:pu le pa, grandfather and father: the forefathers of a people; pulepa ngeina, the tradition of the ancestors) (e:tuipi gei-a teng mite’ ~ ahi uh hi/Shekhem mite’ ~ Hamor | Hamor, the forefather of the Shechemites/a kizui ~ ngeinate zui loin | not following the traditions of the ancestors) (s:pupi/pusawn) (a:tuleta) (r:1.10.5/7.9.28/41.7.5) (q:draft)
pulepate = (t:n) (w:ancestors/forefathers/fathers) (d:pu le pa, grandfather and father, with plural te) (e:amau’ ~ tawh ka thuciamna/amau’ ~ tungah piak dingin a kiciam leitang | the land he had sworn to give their ancestors/amau’ ~ tawh kibang loin | not like their ancestors) (s:pupite/pute/pupa) (a:tuletate) (b:pulepa) (r:3.26.45/6.21.43/19.78.5) (q:draft)
pulh = (t:v) (w:fall off/drop/shed) (d:come off and fall, said of flowers, leaves, unripe fruit and hair) (e:Lopa vul a, paak ~ hi/na oliv gahte uh ~ ding hi | your olives will drop off/theikung panin a tehte a ~ bangin | as leaves fall from the fig tree/a pak zong a ~ thei hi | its flower also falls/A min ma-in a gahte a ~ leenggui | a vine that sheds its fruit before it ripens) (r:23.40.8/3.13.40/5.28.40/23.34.4/60.1.24/18.15.33) (q:draft)
pulhin = (t:v) (w:falling off/dropping) (d:pulh fall off, with the linking ending in, joining it to the next verb; of flowers and leaves) (e:Leenggui panin a tehte ~/a paakte ~ a hoihnateng a bei | its blossoms fall and all its beauty is gone) (b:pulh) (r:23.34.4/59.1.11) (q:draft)
pulhkhia = (t:v) (w:fall off/drop off/fall out) (d:pulh fall off with khia out, away: come loose and drop, as fruit or leaves from a tree) (e:vantung aksite in leitungah a ~ uh hi) (b:pulh) (r:66.6.13/65.1.12) (q:draft)
pulhkhiat = (t:v) (w:drop off/fall off) (d:form of pulhkhia used before bangin) (e:nai lo gahte a ~ bangin) (b:pulhkhia) (r:66.6.13) (q:draft)
pulhmang = (t:v) (w:fall off completely/be shed/drop away) (d:pulh fall off with mang, away or gone: of blossoms, leaves or hair, fall and be lost) (e:a paakte a ~ khin oliv kung/Karmel muala singtehte ~ khinta hi | the leaves on Mount Carmel have all dropped) (r:18.15.33/23.33.9) (q:draft)
pulhmangin = (t:v) (w:fall off completely/be worn bare) (d:pulhmang with the linking ending in; here of hair worn away from the head) (e:a sam nangawn uh ~) (b:pulhmang) (r:26.29.18) (q:draft)
pulnatna = (t:n) (w:plague/pestilence/disease) (d:deadly disease that spreads among many people) (e:~ tawh amaute sisak hi/~ hong dai hi | the plague was checked/a lauhuai ~ panin nangmah hong honkhia ding hi | he will save you from the deadly pestilence/kialpi le ~ zong hong tung ding hi | famines and pestilences will also come) (s:pul/pulpi) (r:19.78.50/19.91.3/19.106.30/42.21.11) (q:draft)
Pulnatna = (t:n) (w:plague) (d:pulnatna, plague, written with a capital at the start of a sentence) (e:~ tawh amaute vatgawpin ka lawnkhia/~ leh sisan luan’na tawh | with plague and bloodshed) (r:4.14.12/26.38.22)
pulnatnate = (t:n) (w:plagues/pestilences/diseases) (d:pulnatna, plague, with the plural te) (e:kialpi leh ~ ka tungsak ding/ama ~ na thuak loh nading uh | so that you will not suffer her plagues) (b:pulnatna) (r:24.24.10/66.18.4) (q:draft)
pulpi = (t:n) (w:plague/great pestilence) (d:pul, plague, with pi, great: a great plague) (e:Tua gamah ~ tungsakin) (s:pulnatna) (b:pul) (r:26.14.19) (q:draft)
pum = (t:adj) (w:round) (d:round like a ball; also used as a counting word for round things such as loaves and fruit) (s:bem/vial)
pum = (t:n) (w:circle)
pumbukah = (t:n) (w:in a furnace) (d:pumbuk furnace or forge with ah in) (e:~ a kihalsan sumngo bang a) (r:66.1.15) (q:draft)
pumguakin = (t:adv) (w:naked/bare) (d:pum, body, with guak, bare, and the adverb ending in: with the body uncovered) (e:a ~ koih ding uh a) (s:tangguakin) (r:66.17.16) (q:draft)
pumkhat = (t:n) (w:one body/one/unity) (d:pum, body, with khat, one: united as one; pumkhat sakhat is one flesh) (e:ka Pa tawh ~ ka hi uh hi/Tua nihte in sakhat ~ a suak ding uh hi | the two will become one flesh/eite khempeuh in ~ bek i hi a | we all are one body/sanggam bangin mite ~ suakin a tutkhawm ciangin | when people live together in unity like brothers) (s:kikhatna) (r:43.10.30/49.5.31/41.10.8/46.10.17/19.133.1) (q:draft)
pumli = (t:num) (w:four) (d:pum, counting word for round things such as fruit, with li four: four olives) (e:singkung hiang dawnah ~ pumnga a om) (r:23.17.6) (q:draft)
pumliin = (t:v) (w:tremble all over/shudder) (d:pum body with liin tremble; written pumliin’ before gawp) (e:~’ gawp ding uh hi | they will tremble violently) (s:pumlin) (r:26.7.18) (q:draft)
pumlin = (t:v) (w:tremble all over/shudder) (d:pum body with lin tremble; written pumlin’ before a) (e:ka zak ciangin ka ~’ a | when I heard, I trembled all over) (s:pumliin) (r:35.3.16) (q:draft)
pumlingawp = (t:v) (w:tremble violently/shudder all over) (d:pumlin, tremble all over, with gawp, severely) (e:silh ding uh a, ~ ding uh hi) (s:linggawp/liinggawp) (b:pumlin) (r:26.7.18) (q:draft)
pumnga = (t:num) (w:five) (d:pum, counting word for round things such as fruit, with nga five: five olives) (e:hiang dawnah pumli ~ a om lai) (r:23.17.6) (q:draft)
pumnih = (t:num) (w:two) (d:pum, counting word for round things such as fruit, with nih two: two olives) (e:sang pen dawnah ~ pumthum) (r:23.17.6) (q:draft)
pumpeeng = (t:n) (c:plant) (w:reed/cane/rod) (d:reed; also a reed stalk used as a staff or a measuring rod) (e:A awnsa ~ kuai tan lo a/Huih mut a, vuang ~ en dingin | to see a reed shaken by the wind/teh nadingin kham ~ khat a tawi hi | he held a golden rod to measure with/Tua ~ tawh khuapi a teh ciangin | when he measured the city with the rod) (s:pumpeng) (r:40.12.20/66.21.15/40.11.7/66.21.16) (q:draft)
pumpeng = (t:n) (c:plant) (w:reed/papyrus) (d:tall water plant growing in marshes) (e:nawng sunga ~ lakah bu hi/Tui om lohna-ah ~ kungte pung thei ahi hiam | can reeds thrive where there is no water/a kisunimsa ~ sukham lo ding a | a bruised reed he will not break/~ tawh kibawl lawh khat | a basket made of papyrus) (s:pumpeeng) (r:18.40.21/18.8.11/23.42.3/2.2.3) (q:draft)
pumpengte = (t:n) (c:plant) (w:reeds) (d:pumpeng, reed, with the plural te) (e:nisuh hiang leh ~ anihin) (s:phaikungte) (b:pumpeng) (r:23.9.14) (q:draft)
pumpi = (t:n) (w:body) (d:the whole body of a person; also the church as the body of Christ, of which the believers are the parts) (e:~ khat bek a om hi/Note in Khazih’ ~ na hi uh hi | you are the body of Christ/Lungnopna in ~ cidamsak a | a heart at peace gives health to the body/~ koi-a om ding hiam | where would the body be) (s:pumtak) (r:46.12.20/46.12.19/46.12.27/20.14.30)
pumpi = (t:n) (w:anatomy)
pumpite = (t:n) (w:bodies) (d:pumpi, body, with the plural te; also of dead bodies) (e:Tua ~ sungah hin’ theihna hu/Vantung ~ a om uh hi | there are heavenly bodies/nangma nasemte’ ~ | the bodies of your servants) (s:luangte) (b:pumpi) (r:26.37.10/46.15.40/19.79.2) (q:draft)
pumsung = (t:n) (w:inside of the body/within) (d:pum, body, with sung, inside: the inside of a person, where desires are at work) (e:note’ ~ a kitoktok niloh ahi hi) (r:59.4.1) (q:draft)
pumtak = (t:n) (w:body) (d:the body itself, contrasted with the clothes that cover it) (e:Puan sangin ~ lianzaw hilo ahi hiam) (s:pumpi) (r:40.6.25) (q:draft)
pumtang = (t:n) (w:whole body/body) (d:a pumtang a phak mi, a man leprous over his whole body) (e:a ~ a phak mi khat) (s:pumpi) (r:42.5.12) (q:draft, low confidence)
pumtangin = (t:adv) (w:with the whole body/all over the body) (d:pumtang, the whole body, with the ending in: in or over the entire body) (e:mizawng khat in a ~ meima vei a/ka ~ ka ci sa gawp hi | my whole body burns with fever/ka mawhna hangin ka ~ ka na hi | because of my sin my whole body is in pain/na ~ hel khua sungah na pai sangin | than for your whole body to go into hell) (b:pumtang) (r:42.16.20/40.5.30/18.30.30/19.38.3) (q:draft)
pumthum = (t:num) (w:three) (d:pum, counting word for round things such as fruit, with thum three: three olives) (e:sang pen dawnah pumnih ~) (r:23.17.6) (q:draft)
pumtuam = (t:v) (w:cover all over/envelop/cover) (d:pum, the whole body, with tuam, cover: cover someone completely, as shame covers a person) (e:maizumna in kei hong ~ khinta hi) (r:19.69.7) (q:draft)
pun = (t:v) (w:increase/multiply/grow in number) (d:form of pung increase used before endings such as na and bang, written pun’ with an apostrophe; pun’na is increase or offspring) (e:Judah minamte a ~’ bangin/siatna bawl khempeuh a ~’ hangin | though all who do evil flourish/Mi ~’na ding deihna-in | wanting the people to increase/na bawngte uh a ~’na | the increase of your herds) (a:kiam) (r:13.4.27/5.28.18/19.92.6/28.4.10) (q:draft)
pung = (t:v) (w:increase/multiply/be fruitful/flourish) (d:grow in number or amount, said of people, herds and crops; also flourish) (e:A bawngte uh ~ a/Ama thupha tawh ante nakpi takin ~ hi | with his blessing they increased greatly/ka bawngte uh ~ hen la | may our cattle increase/Ama hun sungin thumanna ~ hen la | in his days may righteousness flourish/Simeon minamte ~ lo uh hi | the clan of Simeon did not increase) (s:khang) (a:kiam) (r:18.21.10/19.107.38/19.144.14/19.72.7/13.4.27) (q:draft)
pungin = (t:v) (w:increasing/multiplying/being fruitful) (d:pung, increase, with the linking ending in, joining it to the next verb) (e:~ nakpitakin khang uh hi/~ tampi tak pha uh hi | they were fruitful and became very many/~ khang unla | be fruitful and increase/~ leitungah a tam theih nadingin | so that they may multiply and be many on the earth) (s:khangin) (a:kiamin) (b:pung) (r:2.1.7/1.47.27/1.9.7/1.8.17) (q:draft)
pungsak = (t:v) (w:increase/multiply/make numerous) (d:cause to grow in number, pung with the causative sak) (e:ama mite ~ mahmah a/thupha pia-in a mi ka ~ ding hi | I will bless them and increase their people/tuu honpite bangin amau’ innkuante a ~ hi | he increased their families like flocks/A mihing a ganhing in kong ~ ding a | I will multiply your people and animals/amah thupha pia-in ka ~ ding a | I will bless him and make him fruitful) (s:phasak/khangsak) (a:kiamsak) (b:pung) (r:19.105.24/26.37.26/19.107.41/26.36.11/1.17.20) (q:draft)
pungsakin = (t:v) (w:increasing/multiplying/making numerous) (d:pungsak, cause to increase, with the linking ending in that joins it to the next verb) (e:nang hong ~, tampi hong phasakin/thupha hong pia-in hong ~ | blessing you and making you fruitful/note hong ~, tampi kong phasak ding a | I will make you fruitful and multiply you/leh na ganhingte uh ~ | and increasing your livestock) (s:phasakin/khangsakin) (b:pungsak) (r:1.48.4/5.7.13/1.28.3/3.26.9) (q:draft)
pungzaw = (t:v) (w:increase more/increase rather) (d:pung, increase, with zaw, more or rather: increase instead of growing fewer) (e:Kiam kei unla, ~ un) (a:kiam) (b:pung) (r:24.29.6) (q:draft)
punna = (t:n) (w:increase/multiplying/growth in number) (d:pun, increase, with the noun suffix na: growth in number, also the offspring by which a family grows) (e:Mi ~ ding deihna-in/a suan a khakte uh a ~ | the increase of their descendants) (b:pun) (r:28.4.10/18.21.8) (q:draft)
Punon = (t:name) (c:place) (w:Punon) (d:place where the Israelites camped in the wilderness, between Zalmonah and Oboth) (r:4.33.42/4.33.43)
pupa = (t:n) (w:ancestors/forefathers) (d:pu and pa, grandfather and father; pupa ngeina, the custom handed down from the ancestors, tradition) (e:na ~ ngeina uh zui-in/i ~ ngeinate zui lo uh ahi hiam | why do they not follow the traditions of our ancestors/na ~ ngeina uh na zuihna uh hangin | because you follow your tradition) (s:pulepate/pupite) (r:40.15.3/40.15.2/40.15.6) (q:draft)
pupi = (t:n) (w:ancestor) (d:the first ancestor from whom a people or family descends) (e:Eber mi khempeuh’ pian’na ~/Levi in ama ~’ pumpi sungah a om lai hi | Levi was still in the body of his ancestor) (s:pulepa/pusawn) (r:1.10.21/58.7.10)
pupi = (t:n) (w:forebear/forefather) (e:a ~ David khuapi sungah a pate tawh/i ~ David’ thu a teltakin kong gen ding hi | I will tell you plainly about our forefather David/eite’ pianna i ~ Abraham’ thu ngaihsun ni | let us consider our forefather Abraham) (s:pusawn) (r:11.15.24/44.2.29/45.4.1)
pupi = (t:n) (w:root)
pupite = (t:n) (w:ancestors/forefathers/patriarchs) (d:pupi, ancestor, with the plural te: the forefathers or patriarchs of a people) (e:amah a kipan i ~ khempeuh/kamsangte zangin i ~ tungah | to our ancestors through the prophets/~ tungah Pasian’ thuciamna a tungsak dingin | to fulfil the promises of God to the patriarchs/a ~ uh tungah a thuciamna huhau-in | on account of his promise to their patriarchs) (s:pulepate/pute/pupa) (a:tuletate) (b:pupi) (r:44.7.15/45.15.8/58.1.1/45.11.28) (q:draft)
Pur = (t:n) (w:pur/lot) (d:the lot cast by Haman, from which the feast of Purim takes its name) (e:~ kici ai saan-in Judah mite) (r:17.9.24/17.3.7) (q:draft)
Purah = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Purah) (d:servant of Gideon, who went down with him to the camp of Midian) (r:7.7.10/7.7.11)
Purim = (t:name) (w:Purim) (d:Jewish festival recalling the deliverance of the Jews in the days of Esther, named after the pur, the lot cast by Haman) (r:17.9.29/17.9.32)
pusawn = (t:n) (w:forefather/ancestor) (d:pu grandfather with sawn onward: an ancestor further back than the grandfather; compare tusawn) (e:A ~ David a thuman bangin) (s:pupi) (r:11.15.3) (q:draft)
pusuah = (t:v) (w:go out/come out) (d:form of pusuak used before ciangin and nop) (e:na ~ ciangun zong hamsia mah na ngah/na ~ uh ciangin zong thupha na ngah ding | you will also be blessed when you go out/a tum ciang leh a ~ ciangin | when he enters and when he comes out/A topa’ nasemte kiangah Jehu hong ~ ciangin | when Jehu came out to his master’s servants) (a:sungtum/lut) (r:5.28.19/2.28.35/5.28.6/12.9.11) (q:draft)
pusuahin = (t:v) (w:bring out/take out) (d:pusuah with the linking ending in; here of bringing things out and handing them over) (e:kumpipa Sairas in hihte ~) (b:pusuah) (r:15.1.8) (q:draft, low confidence)
pusuahna = (t:n) (w:going out/going/departure) (d:act of going out; paired with sungtumna, coming in: na pusuahna na sungtumna, your going out and coming in) (e:na ~ leh na sungtumna/na ~ na sungtumna-ah nang hong kem ding hi | he will keep you in your going out and coming in/a lutna leh a ~ | its entrances and its exits) (s:paikhiatna) (a:sungtumna/lutna) (b:pusuah) (r:10.3.25/19.121.8/26.43.11) (q:draft)
pusuahpih = (t:v) (w:bring out/lead out) (d:go out together with someone, taking them outside) (e:Tua ciangin amaute ~ a/a leilu lam kongpi tawnin hong ~ a | he brought me out through the north gate/tual puanung-ah hong ~ a | he brought me out to the outer court/nisuahna lam kongpi tawnin hong ~ a | he led me out through the east gate) (s:paikhiatpih) (a:lutpih/tumpih) (b:pusuah) (r:44.16.30/26.42.15/26.47.2/26.46.21) (q:draft)
pusuahsak = (t:v) (w:send out/make go out/put out) (d:pusuah, go out, with the causative sak: make people go out of a place) (e:amaute khempeuh a ~ khit ciangin) (s:paikhiasak) (a:lutsak) (b:pusuah) (r:44.9.40) (q:draft)
pusuak = (t:v) (w:go out/come out) (d:go or come out of a place; also of a voice, fire or words that come out; before ciangin and similar words the form is pusuah) (e:Amah ~ a, nakpi-in a kap hi/amaute mah tawh ~ khawm ding hi | he shall go out together with them/Tokhom sung pan a ~ aw | a voice that came out from the throne/inndeipi kongpi mah-ah ~ ding hi | he shall go out by the same gateway) (s:suakkhia) (a:lut/sungtum) (r:42.22.62/26.46.10/66.19.5/26.46.8) (q:draft)
pusuakin = (t:v) (w:going out/coming out) (d:pusuak, go out, with the linking ending in: going or coming out and then doing the next action) (e:A pa ~ a zol hi/amaute kam sungpan mei ~ | fire comes out of their mouths/Siangtho mun panin siampite hong ~ | the priests came out of the Holy Place/Inn neipa, tua mipa, amaute kiangah ~ | the owner of the house went out to them) (a:lutin/sungtumin) (b:pusuak) (r:42.15.28/66.11.5/14.5.11/7.19.23) (q:draft)
pusuakkhia = (t:v) (w:come out/emerge) (d:pusuak, come out, with khia, out: come out from inside something) (e:sung pan a hong ~ ding a/gimna lianpi sung pan hong ~ mi ahi uh hi | they are the ones who have come out of the great tribulation/leivang panin Hebru mite hong ~ uh hi | the Hebrews are coming out of the holes/kha gilo pawl thum a ~ ka mu hi | I saw three evil spirits come out) (s:suakkhia) (b:pusuak) (r:66.17.8/9.14.11/66.7.14/66.16.13) (q:draft)
pusuaksak = (t:v) (w:bring out/bring forth/produce) (d:pusuak, come out, with the causative sak: make come out, bring out of a store or out of the heart) (e:a pha nate a ~ hi/a huihkua panin huih a ~ hi | he brings out the wind from his storehouses/A lungsim panun gitlohna ~ uh a | from their hearts they bring forth wickedness/a pha lo nate a ~ hi | he brings out things that are not good) (s:suahsak) (b:pusuak) (r:40.12.35/19.73.7/19.135.7) (q:draft)
pusuakte = (t:n) (w:things that come out) (d:pusuak, come out, with the plural te: the things that come out) (e:kam sung panin a ~ pen) (s:paikhiate) (b:pusuak) (r:40.15.18) (q:draft)
pusuakto = (t:v) (w:come up out/come up) (d:pusuak, come out, with to, upward: come up out of something) (e:sa golpi li hong ~ uh hi) (s:dawkto) (b:pusuak) (r:27.7.3) (q:draft)
put = (t:n) (w:look/appearance) (d:seen once after mai face, where the English has their faces are distorted with fear; meaning uncertain, not the word pu grandfather) (e:a mai ~ uh laumai ding) (r:26.27.35) (q:draft, low confidence)
Put = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Put) (d:son of Ham; also the people and land named after him, mentioned with Persia and Cush) (r:1.10.6)
pute = (t:n) (w:ancestors/forefathers/fathers) (d:pu, grandfather, with the plural te: forefathers or ancestors, most often in the pair pute pate, grandfathers and fathers) (e:Na ~ na pate un gampa lakah/Ka ~ uh Abraham, Isaak, leh Israel Pasian | God of our fathers Abraham, Isaac and Israel/~ pate’ tuahsa thute ngaihsun in | consider what the forefathers experienced/a ~ a pate uh Pasian Topa phat in | praising the Lord, the God of their fathers) (s:pupite/pulepate/pupa) (a:suante) (b:pu) (r:43.6.49/13.29.18/18.8.8/13.29.20) (q:draft)
puteek = (t:n) (w:old man/aged man/the aged) (d:pu, grandfather, with teek, old: a man of great age, also said of an aged father and of old people in general) (e:Kei ~ ka hi hi/Na gen uh ~, na pa uh a dam hiam | is your aged father you spoke of well/na innkuan sungah ~ a om loh hun | a time when there is no old man in your family/~ khat in sauveipi nungta ding hi | an old man will live very long) (s:puteekpa/khanghampa) (a:tangval) (r:42.1.18/1.43.27/9.2.31/23.65.20) (q:draft)
puteekpa = (t:n) (w:old man/aged man) (d:puteek, old man, with pa marking a male person: the old man, one particular aged man) (e:Tua ciangin ~ in/amaute in inn neipa ~ kiangah | they said to the old man who owned the house/~’ maitang zahtak-in | honouring the face of the old man/~ hong kici keimah Paul in | I, Paul, who am called an old man) (s:puteek/khanghampa) (a:tangval) (b:puteek) (r:7.19.20/3.19.32/7.19.22/57.1.8) (q:draft)
puteekte = (t:n) (w:old men/the aged/elders) (d:puteek, old man, with the plural te: old men, the aged, the elders of a people) (e:~ khuapi kongpi ah tu nawn lo uh/na ~ un mang manin | your old men will dream dreams/~ hong kithusimsak kei hi | the elders are shown no respect/Tute in ~’ minthan’na lukhu hi a | grandchildren are the crown of the aged/Jerusalem ~ in pau loin leilakah tu uh a | the elders of Jerusalem sit on the ground in silence) (s:putekte/khanghamte/teekte/teeksiate) (a:khangnote/tangvalnote) (b:puteek) (r:25.5.14/29.2.28/25.5.12/20.17.6/25.2.10) (q:draft)
putekte = (t:n) (w:old men/the aged) (d:pu, grandfather, with tek, old, and the plural te, a spelling of puteekte: old men, as in pitek putekte, old women and old men) (e:pitek ~ zong a kipai khiatpih) (s:puteekte/khanghamte/teekte/teeksiate) (a:khangnote) (r:24.6.11) (q:draft)
Puteoli = (t:name) (c:city) (w:Puteoli) (d:port city on the west coast of Italy where Paul landed on his way to Rome) (r:44.28.13)
Puth = (t:name) (c:people) (w:Puthite) (d:clan of Kiriath Jearim in Judah, the Puthites) (r:13.2.53)
Putiel = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Putiel) (d:father-in-law of Eleazar son of Aaron and grandfather of Phinehas) (r:2.6.25)
Puvah = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Puah) (d:son of Issachar, ancestor of the Puite clan) (r:1.46.13/4.26.23)
Ra = (t:see) <Ra-amses>
Ra-amiah = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Raamiah) (d:one of the leaders who returned from exile with Zerubbabel) (e:Nehemiah, Azariah, ~, Nehamani) (r:16.7.6) (q:draft)
Ra-amses = (t:name) (c:city) (w:Rameses) (d:store city in Egypt built by the Israelites for Pharaoh) (e:Pithom leh ~ khuapite lam uh hi) (r:2.1.11) (q:draft)
Raamah = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Raamah) (d:son of Cush and father of Sheba and Dedan; also the trading people descended from him) (r:1.10.7)
Rabbah = (t:name) (c:land/city) (w:Rabbah) (d:capital city of the Ammonites, captured by Joab and David; also a town in the hill country of Judah) (r:10.12.26/6.15.60)
Rabbath = (t:name) (c:city) (w:Rabbah) (d:Rabbah, the capital city of the Ammonites) (r:26.21.20)
Rabbi = (t:n) (w:Rabbi/teacher/master) (d:Jewish title of respect for a teacher, used in speaking to Jesus as Rabbi aw; John explains it as Sia, teacher) (e:~ aw, an ne in/~ aw, koi lai-ah om na hi hiam | Rabbi, where are you staying/~ aw, na samsiat theikung a dinggaw zo hi | Rabbi, the fig tree you cursed has withered/~ aw, nangmah in Pasian’ Tapa na hi hi | Rabbi, you are the Son of God) (s:Sia) (r:43.4.31/40.23.8/43.1.38/41.11.21/43.1.49) (q:draft)
Rabbith = (t:name) (c:place) (w:Rabbith) (d:town in the territory of Issachar) (r:6.19.20)
Rabboni = (t:name) (w:Rabboni) (d:Aramaic title meaning my teacher, used by Mary Magdalene in speaking to the risen Jesus) (r:43.20.16)
Rachael = (t:name) (c:female) (w:Rachel) (d:younger daughter of Laban, wife of Jacob and mother of Joseph and Benjamin) (r:1.29.6/1.35.19)
Rachel = (t:name) (c:female) (w:Rachel) (d:younger daughter of Laban, wife of Jacob and mother of Joseph and Benjamin) (r:1.46.19)
Raddai = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Raddai) (d:fifth son of Jesse, brother of David) (r:13.2.14)
Rafa = (t:see) <Rafa-te>
Rafa-te = (t:name) (c:people) (w:Rapha) (d:Rafa with the plural te, the descendants of Rapha, fifth son of Benjamin) (r:13.8.2) (q:draft)
Rafah = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Raphah) (d:descendant of Saul through Jonathan, son of Binea) (r:13.8.37)
Rafu = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Raphu) (d:man of Benjamin, father of Palti the spy) (r:4.13.9)
Rahab = (t:name) (c:female) (w:Rahab) (d:woman of Jericho who hid the Israelite spies, mother of Boaz; also the name of a sea monster and a poetic name for Egypt) (r:6.2.1/6.6.17)
Raham = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Raham) (d:descendant of Caleb, son of Shema and father of Jorkeam) (e:Shema in ~’ pa hi a) (r:13.2.44) (q:draft)
Rakem = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Rakem) (d:descendant of Manasseh through Makir, brother of Ulam) (r:13.7.16)
Rakhab = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Rekab) (d:Rekab, father of Jehonadab and ancestor of the Rekabites) (r:24.35.16)
Rakkath = (t:name) (c:city) (w:Rakkath) (d:fortified town in the territory of Naphtali) (r:6.19.35)
Rakkon = (t:name) (c:place) (w:Rakkon) (d:place in the territory of Dan near Joppa) (r:6.19.46)
Ram = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Ram) (d:name of several men, among them a son of Hezron of Judah, ancestor of David) (r:8.4.19/13.2.9) (q:draft)
Rama = (t:name) (c:city) (w:Ramah) (d:Ramah, town of Benjamin north of Jerusalem) (e:~ khuamite lau-in ling uh a) (r:23.10.29) (q:draft)
Ramah = (t:name) (c:city) (w:Ramah) (d:name of several towns, among them one in Benjamin and the home town of Samuel) (r:6.19.36/16.11.33)
Ramathaimzofim = (t:name) (c:place) (w:Ramathaimzofim/Ramathaim) (d:home town of Elkanah, father of Samuel, in the hill country of Ephraim) (r:9.1.1)
Ramathlehi = (t:name) (c:place) (w:Ramath Lehi) (d:place in Judah where Samson threw away the jawbone of a donkey) (r:7.15.17)
Ramathmizpeh = (t:name) (c:place) (w:Ramath Mizpah) (d:place on the border of the territory of Gad east of the Jordan) (r:6.13.26)
Rameses = (t:name) (c:male/land) (w:Rameses) (d:district and city in Egypt where the family of Jacob settled and from which the Israelites set out) (r:1.47.11/2.12.37)
Ramiah = (t:name) (c:male) (w:Ramiah) (d:Israelite of the family of Parosh who had married a foreign wife) (r:15.10.25)
Rammah = (t:name) (c:city) (w:Ramah) (d:Ramah, the home town of Samuel) (r:9.19.22)
Ramoth = (t:name) (c:city) (w:Ramoth) (d:name of several towns, among them Ramoth in Gilead and Ramoth in the Negev) (r:5.4.43/9.30.27)
Ramoth-gilead = (t:name) (c:city) (w:Ramoth Gilead) (d:city in Gilead east of the Jordan, fought over by Israel and Aram) (e:kamsang tangvalpa ~ ah pai hi) (r:12.9.4/11.22.29) (q:draft)
